const e=[{id:"fc-ch1-001",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"What are the 5 fundamental principles of British life?",back:`1. Democracy
2. The rule of law
3. Individual liberty
4. Tolerance of those with different faiths and beliefs
5. Participation in community life`,memoryHook:'Acronym "DR. TIP": Democracy, Rule of law, Tolerance, Individual liberty, Participation in community life.'},{id:"fc-ch1-002",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"What 5 shared responsibilities apply to everyone living in the UK?",back:`1. Respect and obey the law
2. Respect the rights of others, including their right to their own opinions
3. Treat others with fairness
4. Look after yourself and your family
5. Look after the area in which you live and the environment`,memoryHook:'Acronym "LOFFE": Law, Opinions/rights of others, Fairness, Family/self, Environment/local area.'},{id:"fc-ch1-003",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"What 5 core freedoms and rights does the UK offer in return for respecting its responsibilities?",back:`1. Freedom of belief and religion
2. Freedom of speech
3. Freedom from unfair discrimination
4. A right to a fair trial
5. A right to join in the election of a government`},{id:"fc-ch1-004",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"What is British society’s stance on extremism and intolerance?",back:"There is NO place in British society for extremism or intolerance of any kind."},{id:"fc-ch1-005",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"How does UK law distinguish between an arranged marriage and a forced marriage?",back:"An arranged marriage (where both parties freely and fully consent) is legal, whereas a forced marriage (where one or both parties do not or cannot consent) is illegal and a criminal offence."},{id:"fc-ch1-006",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"What is the UK legal status of Female Genital Mutilation (FGM), including taking a girl abroad for the procedure?",back:"Both practising FGM in the UK and taking a girl or woman abroad for FGM are illegal and serious criminal offences."},{id:"fc-ch1-007",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"How does UK law treat domestic violence and non-consensual sex within marriage?",back:"Domestic violence and abuse of any kind are serious crimes in the UK; anyone violent towards a partner or family member can be prosecuted, and a husband forcing his wife to have sex can be charged with rape."},{id:"fc-ch1-008",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"On what grounds does UK law protect individuals against unfair discrimination?",back:"UK laws prohibit unfair discrimination based on age, disability, sex, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sexuality, and marital or civil partnership status."},{id:"fc-ch1-009",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:'Exam Trap: Are "buying property", "driving a car", or "free shopping" part of the fundamental principles of British life?',back:"No! Exam distractors love listing lifestyle perks, whereas the fundamental principles are strictly Democracy, Rule of Law, Individual Liberty, Tolerance, and Community Participation."},{id:"fc-ch1-010",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"How many questions are in the official Life in the UK Test, how long does it last, and what is the pass mark?",back:"24 questions in 45 minutes, with a pass mark of 75% (at least 18 correct answers out of 24).",memoryHook:"24 questions (hours in a day), and three-quarters for both: 45 mins (3/4 of an hour) and 18/24 (3/4 = 75%) to pass."},{id:"fc-ch1-011",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"Which edition of the official handbook are all Life in the UK Test questions drawn from?",back:"Life in the United Kingdom: A Guide for New Residents (3rd Edition)."},{id:"fc-ch1-012",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"What English language qualification level is normally required to apply for permanent residence (ILR) or British citizenship?",back:"Speaking and listening proficiency at CEFR level B1 (ESOL Entry Level 3), unless exempt (such as being aged 65+, having a qualifying degree taught in English, or being a citizen of a majority English-speaking country).",memoryHook:'B1 = "Be One" (a citizen), and B (2nd letter) + 1 = ESOL Entry Level 3.'},{id:"fc-ch1-013",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"To whom do new citizens swear or affirm allegiance during a British Citizenship Ceremony?",back:"To His Majesty King Charles III, His Heirs and Successors, according to law."},{id:"fc-ch1-014",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"What is the difference between the Oath of Allegiance and the Affirmation of Allegiance at a Citizenship Ceremony?",back:'The Oath is religious ("I swear by Almighty God..."), while the Affirmation is the non-religious alternative ("I do solemnly, sincerely and truly declare and affirm...") for those who prefer not to swear on God.'},{id:"fc-ch1-015",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"How does the Citizenship Pledge differ from the Oath/Affirmation of Allegiance?",back:"While the Oath/Affirmation pledges allegiance to King Charles III, the Pledge promises loyalty to the United Kingdom and vows to respect its rights and freedoms, uphold its democratic values, observe its laws faithfully, and fulfil duties as a British citizen."},{id:"fc-ch1-016",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:"How does the Life in the UK Test work if you apply for settlement in the Channel Islands or the Isle of Man?",back:"Applicants still take a citizenship/settlement test, which includes local island-specific questions alongside UK questions."},{id:"fc-ch1-017",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-citizenship",front:'What is another official term for becoming a "permanent resident" in the UK?',back:"Settlement, often granted as Indefinite Leave to Remain (ILR), allowing you to live and work in the UK without time limits."},{id:"fc-ch1-018",chapterId:"ch1",subtopicId:"ch1-principles",front:"Does supporting your family and obeying the law excuse ignoring your local community in the UK?",back:"No, being a good neighbour, getting involved in community life, and helping protect your local area and environment are core responsibilities of UK residency."},{id:"fc-ch2-001",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"What is the full official name of the UK, and which four nations form it?",back:"The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, formed of England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland."},{id:"fc-ch2-002",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:'Exam Classic: Which nations are included in "Great Britain", and is Northern Ireland part of Great Britain?',back:'"Great Britain" refers ONLY to England, Scotland, and Wales (the largest island). Northern Ireland is part of the UK, NOT part of Great Britain!',memoryHook:"GB = 3 nations on 1 island (England, Scotland, Wales). Add Northern Ireland across the sea = UK (4)."},{id:"fc-ch2-003",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"What are the capital cities of England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland?",back:`England: London
Scotland: Edinburgh
Wales: Cardiff
Northern Ireland: Belfast`},{id:"fc-ch2-004",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"What is the approximate distance from John O’Groats (north coast of Scotland) to Land’s End (south-west tip of England)?",back:"About 870 miles (approximately 1,400 kilometres).",memoryHook:'"8-7-0 from top to toe": 870 miles (1,400 km) from John O’Groats to Land’s End.'},{id:"fc-ch2-005",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Roughly how wide is Great Britain at its widest point from east to west?",back:"About 300 miles (around 480 km) across.",memoryHook:"3 nations on Great Britain = ~300 miles wide at its widest point."},{id:"fc-ch2-006",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Name three major Scottish cities besides Edinburgh.",back:"Glasgow, Aberdeen, and Dundee (with Inverness in the Highlands and St Andrews on the east coast).",memoryHook:'Acronym "EGAD": Edinburgh (capital), Glasgow, Aberdeen, Dundee.'},{id:"fc-ch2-007",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Name the main cities of Wales alongside its capital Cardiff.",back:"Swansea and Newport (mostly in South Wales, while North Wales includes Bangor/Wrexham)."},{id:"fc-ch2-008",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Name major UK cities in England besides London.",back:"Birmingham, Liverpool, Leeds, Manchester, Sheffield, Bristol, Newcastle upon Tyne, Plymouth, Southampton, and Norwich."},{id:"fc-ch2-009",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"What is the status of the Republic of Ireland in relation to the UK?",back:"The Republic of Ireland (capital: Dublin) is an independent, sovereign country that shares the island of Ireland with Northern Ireland; it is NOT part of the UK."},{id:"fc-ch2-010",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"How is the UK’s population distributed across its four nations?",back:"Distribution is very uneven: England makes up roughly 84% of the total UK population, followed by Scotland (~8%), Wales (~5%), and Northern Ireland (~3%).",memoryHook:"Step up for the smaller nations: NI 3% + Wales 5% = Scotland 8% (total 16%), leaving 84% in England."},{id:"fc-ch2-011",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"union-jack",front:"Which three crosses are combined to create the Union Flag (popularly known as the Union Jack)?",back:`1. Cross of St George (England, upright red cross on white)
2. Cross of St Andrew (Scotland: diagonal white cross on blue)
3. Cross of St Patrick (Ireland, diagonal red cross on white)`,memoryHook:'Acronym "GAP": St George (England), St Andrew (Scotland), St Patrick (Ireland). St David (Wales) is left out.'},{id:"fc-ch2-012",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"union-jack",front:"Why does the Welsh dragon NOT appear on the Union Flag?",back:"Because the Principality of Wales was already united with England when the first Union Flag was created in 1606 (following James VI of Scotland becoming James I of England in 1603)."},{id:"fc-ch2-013",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Describe the official flag of Wales.",back:"A red dragon set across a white (top half) and green (bottom half) horizontal background.",memoryHook:"White on top, green on bottom: picture the Red Dragon standing on green grass under a white sky."},{id:"fc-ch2-014",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Who is the patron saint of Wales, and on what exact date is their feast day celebrated?",back:"St David, celebrated on 1 March.",memoryHook:"1 March = 1st day of spring (when Welsh daffodils bloom), opening the saint calendar: 1 Mar -> 17 Mar -> 23 Apr -> 30 Nov."},{id:"fc-ch2-015",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Who is the patron saint of Northern Ireland, and on what exact date is their day celebrated?",back:"St Patrick, celebrated on 17 March."},{id:"fc-ch2-016",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Who is the patron saint of England, and on what exact date is their day celebrated?",back:"St George, celebrated on 23 April.",memoryHook:"St George = 23 April (the exact same date England’s Shakespeare was born and died)."},{id:"fc-ch2-017",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Who is the patron saint of Scotland, and on what exact date is their day celebrated?",back:"St Andrew, celebrated on 30 November.",memoryHook:"Last day of November (30 Nov): northernmost nation gets the latest, wintriest saint’s day (after 1 Mar, 17 Mar, 23 Apr)."},{id:"fc-ch2-018",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Which UK nations have an official bank (public) holiday for their patron saint’s day?",back:"ONLY Scotland (St Andrew’s Day, 30 Nov, though not all businesses close) and Northern Ireland (St Patrick’s Day, 17 Mar). England and Wales do NOT have bank holidays for St George’s or St David’s Day.",memoryHook:"Diagonal crosses get the day off: only Scotland (St Andrew) and Northern Ireland (St Patrick) have official bank holidays."},{id:"fc-ch2-019",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-symbols",visualId:"national-emblems",front:"Match each of the four UK nations to its traditional national flower/plant emblem.",back:`• England: The Rose (red-and-white Tudor Rose)
• Scotland: The Thistle
• Wales: The Daffodil (the leek is also worn)
• Northern Ireland: The Shamrock`},{id:"fc-ch2-020",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-territories",visualId:"uk-map",front:"What are the Crown Dependencies, and are they part of the United Kingdom?",back:"The Channel Islands (Jersey and Guernsey) and the Isle of Man. They are closely linked to the Crown with their own governments, laws, and tax systems, and are NOT part of the UK."},{id:"fc-ch2-021",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-territories",front:"What is Tynwald, and what world record does it hold?",back:"Tynwald is the parliament of the Isle of Man, and it has the oldest continuous parliament in the world."},{id:"fc-ch2-022",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-territories",front:"What are the British Overseas Territories, and are they part of the UK?",back:"Territories in other parts of the world (such as the Falkland Islands, Gibraltar, St Helena, Bermuda, and the Cayman Islands) that have constitutional links with the UK for defence and foreign affairs, but are NOT part of the UK."},{id:"fc-ch2-023",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-territories",front:"Where are Gibraltar, St Helena, and the Falkland Islands located?",back:`• Gibraltar: southern coast of Spain at the entrance to the Mediterranean
• St Helena: South Atlantic Ocean (where Napoleon was exiled)
• Falkland Islands: South Atlantic Ocean off South America`},{id:"fc-ch2-024",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-territories",front:"What are all the UK coin and banknote denominations of Pound Sterling (£)?",back:`• Coins (8): 1p, 2p, 5p, 10p, 20p, 50p, £1, £2
• Banknotes (4): £5, £10, £20, £50 (£1 = 100 pence)`,memoryHook:"Follow the 1-2-5 pattern all the way up: 1p, 2p, 5p | 10p, 20p, 50p | £1, £2 (8 coins) | £5, £10, £20, £50 (4 notes). Never a 25p coin."},{id:"fc-ch2-025",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-territories",front:"Can banknotes issued by banks in Scotland and Northern Ireland be used in England and Wales?",back:"Yes, Northern Ireland and Scotland have their own banknotes which are valid currency everywhere in the UK, although shops are not legally obliged to accept them."},{id:"fc-ch2-026",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Name four smaller islands off the coast of Great Britain that ARE part of the United Kingdom.",back:"Orkney and Shetland (off northern Scotland), the Western Isles/Hebrides, Anglesey (off North Wales), and the Isle of Wight (off southern England).",memoryHook:'Isle of Man is "its own man" (Crown Dependency, outside the UK with the Channel Islands); Isle of Wight, Anglesey, Orkney, and Shetland are inside the UK.'},{id:"fc-ch2-027",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Which languages are indigenous regional languages spoken in parts of the UK alongside English?",back:"Welsh (widely spoken in Wales and taught in schools), Scottish Gaelic (spoken in the Highlands and Islands of Scotland), and Irish Gaelic / Ulster Scots (in Northern Ireland)."},{id:"fc-ch2-028",chapterId:"ch2",subtopicId:"ch2-nations",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Name two major cities or towns in Northern Ireland.",back:"Belfast (the capital) and Derry/Londonderry (plus Lisburn, Newry, and Armagh)."},{id:"fc-ch4-001",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What is the established state church of England, who is its Supreme Governor, and who is its spiritual leader?",back:"The Church of England (called the Anglican Church internationally and the Episcopal Church in Scotland/US). King Charles III is its Supreme Governor, the Archbishop of Canterbury is its spiritual leader, and 26 senior bishops sit in the House of Lords."},{id:"fc-ch4-002",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"How does the national Church of Scotland differ from the Church of England, and do Wales or Northern Ireland have an established state church?",back:'The Church of Scotland ("the Kirk") is a Presbyterian church governed by ministers and elders whose General Assembly elects a Moderator for 1 year: the Monarch is NOT its head! Neither Wales nor Northern Ireland has an established state church.'},{id:"fc-ch4-003",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"Which religions celebrate Diwali, when does it take place, and which English city is famous for its huge Diwali celebrations?",back:'Celebrated by Hindus and Sikhs in October or November (lasting 5 days), known as the "Festival of Lights" celebrating the victory of good over evil; famously celebrated in Leicester.',memoryHook:'Alliteration: "Lights in Leicester" links the 5-day Festival of Lights (Hindus and Sikhs) to Leicester.'},{id:"fc-ch4-004",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What do the Muslim festivals of Eid al-Fitr and Eid ul Adha celebrate?",back:`• Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan (the month when Muslims fast during daylight hours).
• Eid ul Adha remembers that the prophet Ibrahim was willing to sacrifice his son when God ordered him to.`,memoryHook:"Letter link: Eid al-Fitr = Finish Fasting (end of Ramadan); Eid ul Adha = Abraham/Ibrahim’s willingness to sacrifice."},{id:"fc-ch4-005",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What does the Jewish festival of Hanukkah celebrate, and how long does it last?",back:"Celebrated in November or December for 8 days, remembering the Jews’ struggle for religious freedom when a single day’s supply of oil miraculously burned for 8 days (marked by lighting a candle each night on an 8-candle menorah).",memoryHook:"H-A-N-U-K-K-A-H has 8 letters for 8 days and 8 candles."},{id:"fc-ch4-006",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What is Vaisakhi, who celebrates it, and on what date?",back:"A Sikh festival celebrated on 14 April each year with parades, dancing, and singing, marking the founding of the Sikh community known as the Khalsa.",memoryHook:'Both "V" celebrations fall on the 14th: Valentine’s = 14 Feb; Vaisakhi = 14 April (14/4).'},{id:"fc-ch4-007",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What are Lent, Shrove Tuesday, and Ash Wednesday in the Christian calendar?",back:'Lent is the 40-day period of reflection and fasting before Easter, beginning on Ash Wednesday. The day before Lent starts is Shrove Tuesday ("Pancake Day"), when people traditionally eat pancakes to use up rich foods like eggs, fat, and milk.'},{id:"fc-ch4-008",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What are the dates of Christmas Day and Boxing Day, and which Easter days are UK public holidays?",back:"Christmas Day is 25 December (birth of Jesus Christ) and Boxing Day is 26 December. At Easter (March/April), both Good Friday (Jesus’s death on the cross) and Easter Monday are UK bank holidays."},{id:"fc-ch4-009",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"Exam Favourite: What is the strict traditional time rule on April Fool’s Day (1 April)?",back:"People play jokes on each other ONLY until 12:00 midday (noon), even TV and newspapers sometimes run spoof stories before noon!"},{id:"fc-ch4-010",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"When are Mothering Sunday, Father’s Day, and Valentine’s Day celebrated in the UK?",back:`• Valentine’s Day: 14 February
• Mothering Sunday (Mother’s Day): the Sunday 3 weeks before Easter
• Father’s Day: the 3rd Sunday in June`,memoryHook:"Rule of 3s for parents: Mothering Sunday = 3 weeks before Easter (not May); Father’s Day = 3rd Sunday in June."},{id:"fc-ch4-011",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What event does Bonfire Night on 5 November commemorate, and what happens on Halloween (31 October)?",back:"Bonfire Night (5 November) marks the failure of Guy Fawkes and Catholic plotters to blow up Parliament and King James I in the 1605 Gunpowder Plot, celebrated with fireworks and bonfires. Halloween (31 October) is an ancient pagan festival where people dress up and carve lanterns.",memoryHook:'"Remember, remember the 5th of November" (and the 5 in 1605): Guy Fawkes’s Gunpowder Plot.'},{id:"fc-ch4-012",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"How is Remembrance Day observed on 11 November, and where is the main memorial in London?",back:"People wear red poppies (inspired by WWI battlefields), observe a 2-minute silence at 11:00 am on 11 November (originally marking the 1918 WWI Armistice), and wreaths are laid at the Cenotaph in Whitehall, London.",memoryHook:"Triple 11: 11th hour of the 11th day of the 11th month (11:00 am on 11 Nov)."},{id:"fc-ch4-013",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"What is New Year’s Eve called in Scotland, and what extra bank holiday does Scotland enjoy at New Year?",back:"New Year’s Eve (31 December) is called Hogmanay in Scotland (a bigger celebration historically than Christmas there), and 2 January is also a public holiday in Scotland!"},{id:"fc-ch4-014",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-religion-customs",front:"When is Burns Night celebrated, who does it honour, and what traditional dish is served?",back:'Celebrated on 25 January in Scotland (and worldwide) to honour Scottish poet Robert Burns ("The Bard", author of Auld Lang Syne), featuring a supper of haggis (stuffed sheep’s stomach with oatmeal, spices, and offal) piped in by bagpipes.',memoryHook:"Date link: Burns Night (25 Jan) is exactly one month after Christmas Day (25 Dec)."},{id:"fc-ch4-015",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:'Where can the modern game of golf be traced back to, which town is called the "home of golf", and what is special about The Open Championship?',back:'Modern golf dates back to 15th-century Scotland; St Andrews in Fife is known worldwide as the "home of golf", and The Open Championship is the ONLY golf "Major" held outside the United States.'},{id:"fc-ch4-016",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"How do Rugby Union and Rugby League differ, and which 6 countries contest the Six Nations Championship?",back:"Rugby originated at Rugby School in England. Rugby Union (15 players per side) and Rugby League (13 players per side) have different rules. The famous Rugby Union tournament is the Six Nations: England, Scotland, Wales, Ireland (all-island team), France, and Italy.",memoryHook:"Union brings MORE players together (15 per side) than League (13 per side)."},{id:"fc-ch4-017",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"What makes the Wimbledon Championships unique in world tennis?",back:"Held every summer at the All England Club in south-west London, Wimbledon is the oldest tennis tournament in the world and the only Grand Slam played on grass courts."},{id:"fc-ch4-018",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"Compare the UK’s two most famous horse-racing events: Royal Ascot and the Grand National.",back:`• Royal Ascot: a prestigious 5-day flat-racing event in Berkshire near Windsor Castle, attended by King Charles III and members of the Royal Family (famous for elaborate hats).
• The Grand National: the famous steeplechase jump race held at Aintree near Liverpool (plus a Scottish Grand National at Ayr).`},{id:"fc-ch4-019",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"What are The Boat Race, Cowes Week, and Silverstone famous for in UK sport?",back:`• The Boat Race: annual rowing race on the River Thames in London between Oxford and Cambridge Universities.
• Cowes Week: major sailing regatta off the Isle of Wight.
• Silverstone: circuit in Northamptonshire that hosts the British Formula 1 Grand Prix.`},{id:"fc-ch4-020",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"Who was Bobby Moore (1941–1993), and what historic sporting triumph did he captain?",back:"Bobby Moore captained the England men’s football team that won the FIFA World Cup in 1966 (hosted in England)."},{id:"fc-ch4-021",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"What world athletics milestone did Sir Roger Bannister (1929–2018) achieve in 1954?",back:"In 1954 (in Oxford), Sir Roger Bannister became the first man in the world to run a mile in under 4 minutes!",memoryHook:"Number link: under 4 minutes in 1954 (both end in 4)."},{id:"fc-ch4-022",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"What sporting achievements made Sir Jackie Stewart and the duo Jayne Torvill & Christopher Dean famous?",back:`• Sir Jackie Stewart (born 1939): Scottish racing driver who won the Formula 1 World Championship 3 times.
• Jayne Torvill & Christopher Dean: won Olympic gold for ice dancing at the 1984 Sarajevo Winter Olympics with perfect 6.0 scores skating to Ravel’s Bolero.`},{id:"fc-ch4-023",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"What Olympic records belong to rower Sir Steve Redgrave and runner Dame Kelly Holmes?",back:`• Sir Steve Redgrave: won gold medals in rowing at 5 consecutive Olympic Games (1984–2000)!
• Dame Kelly Holmes: won two gold medals, for both the 800m and 1500m, at the 2004 Athens Olympics.`},{id:"fc-ch4-024",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"Who are Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson and Dame Ellen MacArthur?",back:`• Baroness Tanni Grey-Thompson (born in Cardiff): wheelchair racer who won 16 Paralympic medals (including 11 golds!) and 6 London Marathons.
• Dame Ellen MacArthur: yachtswoman who in 2004 became the fastest person to sail solo nonstop around the world.`},{id:"fc-ch4-025",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"Distinguish British cycling legends Sir Chris Hoy and Sir Bradley Wiggins.",back:`• Sir Chris Hoy: Scottish track cyclist who won 6 Olympic gold medals and 1 silver.
• Sir Bradley Wiggins: road and track cyclist who in 2012 became the FIRST Briton ever to win the Tour de France (and won 8 Olympic medals including 5 golds).`},{id:"fc-ch4-026",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"What historic London 2012 athletics feats were achieved by Sir Mo Farah and Dame Jessica Ennis-Hill?",back:`• Sir Mo Farah (born in Somalia): distance runner who became the first Briton to win Olympic gold in the 5,000m and 10,000m (achieving the "double" at London 2012 and repeating it at Rio 2016).
• Dame Jessica Ennis-Hill: won the 2012 Olympic gold medal in the heptathlon (7 track and field events).`},{id:"fc-ch4-027",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-sports",front:"Why is Scottish tennis star Sir Andy Murray’s 2013 Wimbledon victory historically famous?",back:"After winning the 2012 US Open and 2012 Olympic singles gold, Andy Murray won Wimbledon in 2013: becoming the FIRST British man to win the Wimbledon singles title since Fred Perry in 1936 (77 years)!"},{id:"fc-ch4-028",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Which famous novels were written by Jane Austen (1775–1817) and Charles Dickens (1812–1870)?",back:`• Jane Austen: Pride and Prejudice and Sense and Sensibility (focused on marriage, family relationships, and Regency society).
• Charles Dickens: Oliver Twist and Great Expectations (vivid Victorian stories with everyday expressions like "Scrooge").`},{id:"fc-ch4-029",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Match Victorian/Edwardian authors Robert Louis Stevenson, Thomas Hardy, and Sir Arthur Conan Doyle to their famous works.",back:`• Robert Louis Stevenson: Treasure Island, Kidnapped, and Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde.
• Thomas Hardy: rural Dorset/"Wessex" novels Far from the Madding Crowd and Jude the Obscure.
• Sir Arthur Conan Doyle: Scottish doctor who created detective Sherlock Holmes (1887).`},{id:"fc-ch4-030",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Which books made J.R.R. Tolkien and J.K. Rowling world-famous, and what poll did Tolkien win in 2003?",back:`• J.R.R. Tolkien wrote The Lord of the Rings, which was voted the country’s best-loved novel in the BBC’s Big Read poll in 2003!
• J.K. Rowling wrote the hugely successful Harry Potter series of children’s/fantasy books.`},{id:"fc-ch4-031",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Who was Dylan Thomas (1914–1953), and what are his best-known works?",back:'A celebrated Welsh poet and writer (born in Swansea) who wrote the radio play Under Milk Wood (first performed after his death in 1953) and the poem "Do Not Go Gentle into That Good Night".'},{id:"fc-ch4-032",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Compare British landscape painters Joseph Turner (1775–1851) and John Constable (1776–1837).",back:`• Joseph (J.M.W.) Turner: influential Romantic painter of dramatic light and seascapes (e.g. The Fighting Temeraire), considered a forerunner of modern art (the Turner Prize is named after him).
• John Constable: famous for tranquil English countryside scenes, especially Dedham Vale on the Suffolk–Essex border (The Hay Wain).`,memoryHook:"Alliteration: Turner = Turbulent seas and dramatic light; Constable = Calm Countryside (Dedham Vale)."},{id:"fc-ch4-033",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"What artistic styles are sculptor Henry Moore (1898–1986) and painter David Hockney (born 1937) famous for?",back:`• Henry Moore: English sculptor and artist best known for large cast bronze abstract sculptures (often reclining figures with hollow spaces).
• David Hockney: major contributor to the 1960s "Pop Art" movement and still influential today.`},{id:"fc-ch4-034",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Who designed the rebuilt St Paul’s Cathedral, and which two Gothic Revival architects designed the 19th-century Houses of Parliament?",back:`• Sir Christopher Wren designed the new St Paul’s Cathedral after the 1666 Great Fire of London.
• In the 19th century, Sir Charles Barry designed the Gothic Revival Houses of Parliament building, while Augustus Pugin designed its ornate Gothic interiors, furniture, and clock tower.`,memoryHook:"Alliteration for Westminster: Barry = Building exterior; Pugin = Pretty Gothic interiors and clock tower."},{id:"fc-ch4-035",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:'Which landmarks did 20th-century architect Sir Edwin Lutyens design, and who was Lancelot "Capability" Brown?',back:`• Sir Edwin Lutyens designed New Delhi (seat of government in British India) and war memorials across the globe, notably the Cenotaph in Whitehall, London.
• Lancelot "Capability" Brown was an 18th-century landscape designer who created natural-looking grass, trees, and lakes around country houses (saying estates had "great capabilities").`},{id:"fc-ch4-036",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Match composers George Frideric Handel, Gustav Holst, and Sir Edward Elgar to their famous works.",back:`• Handel (German-born, became a British citizen in 1727): Messiah, Water Music (for King George I), and Music for the Royal Fireworks.
• Gustav Holst: The Planets.
• Sir Edward Elgar (born in Worcester): Pomp and Circumstance Marches (March No. 1 = "Land of Hope and Glory", played at the Last Night of the Proms!).`},{id:"fc-ch4-037",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Which hit musicals were written by Lord Andrew Lloyd Webber, and who preceded him in Victorian comic opera?",back:`• Andrew Lloyd Webber composed music for The Phantom of the Opera, Cats, Evita, and Jesus Christ Superstar (many with Tim Rice).
• In the 19th century, Gilbert and Sullivan wrote comic operas like HMS Pinafore, The Pirates of Penzance, and The Mikado.`},{id:"fc-ch4-038",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"What are the BBC Proms, and where is their famous finale held?",back:'An 8-week summer season of daily orchestral classical music concerts organised by the BBC since 1927, held in venues including the Royal Albert Hall in London and culminating in the televised "Last Night of the Proms".'},{id:"fc-ch4-039",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Match these 5 major UK cultural awards to their discipline: Booker Prize, Turner Prize, Mercury Prize, BRIT Awards, and Laurence Olivier Awards.",back:`• Booker Prize: Literature / best fiction novel
• Turner Prize: Contemporary visual art (held at Tate Britain)
• Mercury Prize: Best album from the UK and Ireland
• BRIT Awards: Annual British popular music awards
• Laurence Olivier Awards: Theatre (named after actor Lord Olivier)`,memoryHook:"Name clues: BOOKer = Books; TURNER = painter Turner (Art); MERCURY = think Freddie Mercury (Music album); OLIVIER = actor Laurence Olivier (Theatre)."},{id:"fc-ch4-040",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Which crucial 1930s aviation and defence technologies were developed in Britain by Sir Frank Whittle and Sir Robert Watson-Watt?",back:`• Sir Frank Whittle (RAF officer): developed the jet engine in the 1930s.
• Sir Robert Watson-Watt (Scottish physicist): proposed and developed radar (using radio waves to detect enemy aircraft) in 1935.`,memoryHook:'Alliteration for the two 1930s "W" inventors: Whittle = Whoosh (jet engine); Watson-Watt = Waves/Watts (radar).'},{id:"fc-ch4-041",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Match pioneers John Logie Baird, Alan Turing, and Sir Tim Berners-Lee to their world-changing inventions.",back:`• John Logie Baird (Scottish): developed television in the 1920s (and made the first transatlantic TV broadcast in 1928).
• Alan Turing: invented the theoretical Turing Machine (1930s), mathematical father of modern computer science, and helped crack German Enigma codes at Bletchley Park in WWII.
• Sir Tim Berners-Lee: invented the World Wide Web (first successful webpage communication on 25 December 1990).`},{id:"fc-ch4-042",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"What medical breakthroughs are linked to Sir Peter Mansfield and Francis Crick?",back:`• Sir Peter Mansfield: British physicist who co-invented the MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) scanner for non-invasive internal scans.
• Francis Crick: co-discovered the double-helix structure of the DNA molecule in 1953 (alongside James Watson, Maurice Wilkins, and Rosalind Franklin at Cambridge and London).`,memoryHook:"Sound link: Mansfield = Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI); Crick = Cracked the DNA double helix (1953)."},{id:"fc-ch4-043",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"What biological milestones were achieved by Sir Ian Wilmut & Keith Campbell (1996) and Patrick Steptoe & Sir Robert Edwards (1978)?",back:`• Sir Ian Wilmut & Keith Campbell (at the Roslin Institute near Edinburgh): cloned Dolly the Sheep in 1996, the first mammal ever cloned from an adult cell!
• Patrick Steptoe (gynaecologist) & Sir Robert Edwards (physiologist): pioneered IVF (in-vitro fertilisation), leading to the birth of the world’s first "test-tube baby" (Louise Brown) in Oldham in 1978.`,memoryHook:"Word link: Wilmut = Woolly sheep (cloned Dolly, 1996); Steptoe = baby’s first steps (pioneered IVF with Edwards, 1978)."},{id:"fc-ch4-044",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",visualId:"uk-map",front:"How many National Parks are in England, Wales, and Scotland, which is England’s largest, and what is Wastwater?",back:"There are 15 National Parks across England, Wales, and Scotland. The Lake District in north-west England is England’s largest national park (885 sq miles), and its lake Wastwater was voted Britain’s favourite view by TV viewers in 2007."},{id:"fc-ch4-045",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Where are Snowdonia National Park and Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park located?",back:`• Snowdonia is in North Wales, its highest mountain is Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa), the highest peak in England and Wales.
• Loch Lomond and the Trossachs is in west-central Scotland: Loch Lomond is the largest expanse of fresh water by surface area in mainland Britain.`},{id:"fc-ch4-046",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Where is the Eden Project located, and what is it famous for?",back:'Located in Cornwall (south-west England); its giant greenhouse "biomes" house plants from all over the world, and the charity runs environmental and social projects internationally.'},{id:"fc-ch4-047",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",visualId:"uk-map",front:"Where is the Giant’s Causeway located, and how was it formed?",back:"Located on the north-east coast of Northern Ireland; it is a dramatic land formation of interlocking columns made from volcanic basalt lava around 50 million years ago."},{id:"fc-ch4-048",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",front:'Exam Trap: What is "Big Ben", and what is the official name of the clock tower it hangs inside?',back:'"Big Ben" is strictly the nickname for the great bell of the striking clock at the Houses of Parliament (though many people call the clock/tower Big Ben). The clock tower itself was renamed Elizabeth Tower in 2012 to mark Queen Elizabeth II’s Diamond Jubilee.',memoryHook:'Rhyme: "Big Ben is the BELL; Elizabeth Tower is the SHELL."'},{id:"fc-ch4-049",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",front:"When and why was the National Trust founded, and how is it operated?",back:"Founded in 1895 by three volunteers (Octavia Hill, Sir Robert Hunter, and Canon Hardwicke Rawnsley), both the National Trust (England, Wales & NI) and the National Trust for Scotland are charities backed by volunteers and members that preserve historic buildings, coastlines, and countryside."},{id:"fc-ch4-050",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-landmarks",front:"What are the Tower of London, the London Eye, and Edinburgh Castle famous for?",back:`• Tower of London: built by William the Conqueror after 1066; houses the Crown Jewels with tours led by Yeoman Warders ("Beefeaters").
• London Eye: 443-foot (135m) observation wheel on the South Bank of the Thames built for the 2000 Millennium.
• Edinburgh Castle: historic fortress dominating Edinburgh’s skyline on volcanic rock.`},{id:"fc-ch4-051",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"What are the Edinburgh Festival Fringe, Glastonbury, and the National Eisteddfod of Wales?",back:`• Edinburgh Festival Fringe: biggest annual arts/comedy/theatre showcase in Scotland every summer.
• Glastonbury: iconic major outdoor contemporary music festival.
• National Eisteddfod of Wales: annual Welsh-language cultural festival of literature, music, and performance.`},{id:"fc-ch4-052",chapterId:"ch4",subtopicId:"ch4-arts-culture",front:"Who invented the Hovercraft and the ATM cash machine in Britain?",back:`• Sir Christopher Cockerell invented the Hovercraft in the 1950s.
• James Goodfellow (PIN/card system) and John Shepherd-Barron (first cash dispenser installed at Barclays in Enfield, north London, 1967) pioneered the ATM cash machine.`,memoryHook:'Wordplay: a cockerel flaps just above the ground -> Cockerell (Hovercraft); "Cash Baron" -> Shepherd-Barron (ATM, with Goodfellow).'},{id:"fc-ch5-001",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-democracy-constitution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:'Why is the UK said to have an "unwritten" (uncodified) constitution?',back:"Because British constitutional rules, Acts of Parliament, common law, and historic conventions have evolved over centuries and have NEVER been written down in one single formal constitutional document."},{id:"fc-ch5-002",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-democracy-constitution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:'Who is the current Head of State of the UK, and what does "constitutional monarchy" mean?',back:"His Majesty King Charles III is the Head of State. In a constitutional monarchy, the King reigns symbolically and represents the nation, but does NOT rule politically: decisions on government policies and laws are made by the Prime Minister, Cabinet, and elected Parliament."},{id:"fc-ch5-003",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-democracy-constitution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"What key constitutional duties does King Charles III carry out in relation to Parliament and the Prime Minister?",back:`1. Invites the leader of the party with the majority of MPs in the House of Commons to become Prime Minister
2. Holds regular weekly meetings with the PM (to advise, warn, and encourage)
3. Opens each new parliamentary session with the King’s Speech (written by the government)
4. Grants Royal Assent to bills passed by Parliament`},{id:"fc-ch5-004",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-democracy-constitution",front:"What is the UK National Anthem?",back:'"God Save the King", played at important national occasions and in the presence of His Majesty King Charles III.'},{id:"fc-ch5-005",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"How many Members of Parliament (MPs) sit in the House of Commons, and how are they elected?",back:'650 MPs, each representing one parliamentary constituency across the UK: elected under the "First Past the Post" system (whichever candidate wins the most votes in that constituency wins the seat).'},{id:"fc-ch5-006",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:'At least how often must a UK Westminster General Election be held, and when is a "by-election" held?',back:'A General Election must be held at least every 5 years (though it can be called earlier). If an individual MP dies or resigns between general elections, a "by-election" is held just in that constituency.'},{id:"fc-ch5-007",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Who sits in the House of Lords, and which chamber is more powerful, the Commons or the Lords?",back:"The House of Lords (unelected) comprises life peers (appointed by the Monarch on the PM’s advice for expertise/public service), 26 senior Church of England bishops, and a small number of hereditary peers. The elected House of Commons is MORE powerful and can ultimately overrule the Lords!"},{id:"fc-ch5-008",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Who is the Speaker of the House of Commons, and how are they chosen?",back:"The Speaker is an MP elected by fellow MPs in a secret ballot to chair debates, enforce Commons rules, and remain strictly politically neutral."},{id:"fc-ch5-009",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"On which day of the week does Prime Minister’s Questions (PMQs) take place when Parliament is sitting?",back:"Every Wednesday (for 30 minutes at noon), when MPs and the Leader of the Opposition question the Prime Minister directly.",memoryHook:"Mid-week at midday: PMQs is every Wednesday at 12:00 noon."},{id:"fc-ch5-010",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"In the Prime Minister’s Cabinet (~20 senior ministers), what are the roles of the Chancellor of the Exchequer, Home Secretary, and Foreign Secretary?",back:`• Chancellor of the Exchequer: responsible for the economy, tax, and public finances
• Home Secretary: responsible for crime, policing, national security, and immigration
• Foreign Secretary: responsible for managing relationships with foreign countries`},{id:"fc-ch5-011",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"What is the Official Opposition and the Shadow Cabinet?",back:'The second-largest party in the House of Commons is the Official Opposition. Its leader (Leader of the Opposition) appoints senior opposition MPs to the "Shadow Cabinet" to challenge government ministers and put forward alternative policies.'},{id:"fc-ch5-012",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:'What are parliamentary "Whips", and what is "Hansard"?',back:`• Whips: MPs or peers appointed by party leaders to maintain party discipline and ensure MPs attend and vote along party lines.
• Hansard: the official word-for-word (verbatim) written report of all Parliamentary debates and proceedings, free to read in libraries and online.`,memoryHook:'"Whips" comes from hunting ("whipping in" the pack so MPs vote in line); Hansard = "Heard and Hard-copy" verbatim record.'},{id:"fc-ch5-013",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",front:"How are UK civil servants appointed, and do they change when a new political party wins an election?",back:"Civil servants are chosen on merit through open competition, are politically neutral (bound by core values of integrity, honesty, objectivity, and impartiality), and stay in post to serve whichever elected government takes office."},{id:"fc-ch5-014",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",front:"Who is eligible to vote in a UK General Election, and who can stand as a candidate for MP?",back:`• Voting (aged 18+ on the electoral register): UK citizens, qualifying Irish citizens, and qualifying Commonwealth citizens resident in the UK.
• Standing as an MP (aged 18+): UK, Irish, or qualifying Commonwealth citizens, EXCEPT members of the armed forces, civil servants, police, and people convicted of certain criminal offences.`},{id:"fc-ch5-015",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Where is the Scottish Parliament based, how many members does it have, and what can it pass laws on?",back:"Based at Holyrood in Edinburgh with 129 MSPs (Members of the Scottish Parliament, opened in 1999). It can pass laws on domestic matters not reserved to Westminster: including health, education, civil and criminal law, planning, and income-tax powers.",memoryHook:"Going north, devolved seats go UP by ~30+: Wales 60 -> NI 90 -> Scotland 129 MSPs (Holyrood)."},{id:"fc-ch5-016",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Where does Senedd Cymru (the Welsh Parliament) sit, how many members does it have, and what areas does it cover?",back:"Based in Cardiff with 60 MSs (Members of the Senedd; first opened as the Welsh Assembly in 1999). It legislates on devolved areas including education and training, health and social services, agriculture, housing, transport, and the Welsh language.",memoryHook:"Going north, devolved seats go UP by ~30+: Wales 60 MSs (Senedd) -> NI 90 -> Scotland 129."},{id:"fc-ch5-017",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Where does the Northern Ireland Assembly sit, how many members does it have, and how is power shared?",back:"Based at Stormont in Belfast with 90 MLAs (Members of the Legislative Assembly, established after the 1998 Belfast/Good Friday Agreement). It operates with a power-sharing executive across main parties; the UK government has the power to suspend the Assembly if leaders cannot work together.",memoryHook:"Going north, devolved seats go UP by ~30+: Wales 60 -> NI 90 MLAs (Stormont) -> Scotland 129."},{id:"fc-ch5-018",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Exam Favourite: What type of electoral system is used for the Scottish Parliament, Senedd Cymru, and the Northern Ireland Assembly?",back:"Forms of Proportional Representation (PR), NOT First Past the Post, meaning seats more closely match each party’s share of votes, so a single party rarely wins an outright majority alone."},{id:"fc-ch5-019",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:'Which "reserved" UK-wide matters remain exclusively controlled by the Westminster Parliament rather than devolved governments?',back:"Defence, foreign affairs, immigration, general taxation/currency, and social security.",memoryHook:'Think "4 Bs + Foreign affairs" reserved to Westminster: Borders (immigration), Battles (defence), Banknotes (tax/currency), Benefits (social security).'},{id:"fc-ch5-020",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",front:"What is the Commonwealth, how many member states belong to it, and does it have legal power over members?",back:"A voluntary association of 56 independent countries (mostly former British Empire nations, though membership isn’t limited to them) promoting democracy, good government, and rule of law. King Charles III is its symbolic Head. It has NO legal power over members, though it can suspend membership.",memoryHook:"Consecutive digits 5-6 = 56 Commonwealth members (compare: ~46 in Council of Europe, 190+ in UN)."},{id:"fc-ch5-021",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",front:"What is the United Nations (UN), and what special status does the UK hold on the UN Security Council?",back:"Set up after WWII with over 190 member countries to prevent war and promote international peace and security. The UN Security Council has 15 members: and the UK is one of ONLY 5 permanent members (alongside the USA, France, Russia, and China)."},{id:"fc-ch5-022",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",front:"What is the core purpose of NATO (the North Atlantic Treaty Organization)?",back:"A group of European and North American countries (including the UK) that have agreed to help defend each other militarily if any member is attacked, aiming to maintain peace among allies."},{id:"fc-ch5-023",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-devolution",front:"Huge Exam Trap: What is the Council of Europe, is it part of the EU, and what famous convention did it create?",back:"The Council of Europe (around 46–47 member countries, including the UK!) is completely SEPARATE from the European Union. It has no power to make laws directly, but draws up conventions: most famously the European Convention on Human Rights (ECHR), given force in UK courts via the Human Rights Act 1998.",memoryHook:"Council of Europe ≠ EU: the UK left the EU, NEVER left the Council of Europe (which created the ECHR)."},{id:"fc-ch5-024",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"What is the difference between Criminal Law and Civil Law in the UK?",back:`• Criminal Law: offences against laws passed by Parliament / society (e.g. theft, violence, carrying a weapon, illegal drugs), prosecuted by the Crown Prosecution Service (CPS) or Procurator Fiscal in Scotland.
• Civil Law: disputes between individuals or groups (e.g. housing/landlord disputes, consumer rights, faulty goods, employment unfair dismissal, debt, family law).`},{id:"fc-ch5-025",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Compare Magistrates’ Courts and Crown Courts in England, Wales, and Northern Ireland.",back:`• Magistrates’ Court: hears most minor criminal offences; decided by unpaid community volunteers called Magistrates / Justices of the Peace (JPs): who need no legal qualifications and receive training, or a paid District Judge (no jury).
• Crown Court: hears serious criminal offences before a judge and a 12-member jury.`,memoryHook:"Alliteration: Minor offences = Magistrates’ Court (unpaid JPs, no jury); serious Crimes = Crown Court (judge + 12 jurors)."},{id:"fc-ch5-026",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Exam Classic: How does the criminal court and jury system in Scotland differ from England, Wales, and Northern Ireland?",back:'In Scotland, minor offences go to Justice of the Peace Courts, while serious offences go to Sheriff Courts or the High Court of Justiciary. A Scottish criminal jury has 15 members (not 12!) and can reach THREE possible verdicts: Guilty, Not Guilty, or "Not Proven"!',memoryHook:'Scottish jury = 15 (same size as a Rugby Union XV, vs 12 in E/W/NI), plus a 3rd verdict: "Not Proven".'},{id:"fc-ch5-027",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"How are accused young people aged 10 to 17 dealt with in England, Wales, and Northern Ireland versus Scotland?",back:`• England, Wales & NI: Youth Courts (heard by up to 3 specially trained magistrates or a District Judge; closed to the public; media cannot publish the youth’s name or photo; parents/carers expected to attend; most serious crimes go to Crown Court).
• Scotland: uses the Children’s Hearings System for children and young people.`},{id:"fc-ch5-028",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"What are the financial claim limits for the informal Small Claims Procedure in England & Wales versus Scotland & Northern Ireland?",back:`• England and Wales: claims under £10,000
• Scotland and Northern Ireland: claims up to £5,000 (can also be issued online via Money Claims Online).`,memoryHook:"Small Claims: £10,000 in England & Wales; HALF (£5,000) in Scotland & Northern Ireland."},{id:"fc-ch5-029",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"Is the UK judiciary independent of the government, and what is the highest court in the UK?",back:"Yes, judges are strictly independent of the government; politicians cannot interfere in trials or tell judges how to decide cases. If judges find a public body acted unlawfully, they can order it to change practices. The highest court of appeal is the UK Supreme Court."},{id:"fc-ch5-030",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"Who are Police and Crime Commissioners (PCCs), and what do they do?",back:"Directly elected officials in England and Wales chosen by local voters to set local policing priorities, set the local police budget, and appoint (or dismiss) the Chief Constable."},{id:"fc-ch5-031",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"Which telephone numbers do you dial in the UK for an urgent emergency versus a non-emergency police report?",back:`• 999 (or 112), free of charge for urgent emergencies requiring police, fire, ambulance, or coastguard.
• 101: for non-emergency police matters.`},{id:"fc-ch5-032",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"2026 Rule Check: What is the minimum legal age to play the UK National Lottery or buy scratchcards?",back:"18 years old (raised from 16 to 18 in 2021, anyone under 18 cannot buy National Lottery tickets or scratchcards!).",memoryHook:"All major adult milestones align at 18: voting, buying alcohol/tobacco, and playing the National Lottery (never pick 16)."},{id:"fc-ch5-033",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"What are the UK legal age rules on buying alcohol and tobacco, including the meal exception for 16- and 17-year-olds?",back:"It is illegal to sell alcohol or tobacco products to anyone under 18. Exception: people aged 16 or 17 can drink wine, beer, or cider (NOT spirits!) with a meal in a hotel or restaurant as long as they are accompanied by someone aged 18 or over who buys it."},{id:"fc-ch5-034",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"At what vehicle age is an annual MOT test legally required in the UK, and what else must a driver have?",back:"Cars and motorcycles over 3 years old must pass an annual MOT road-safety and emissions test. Drivers must also hold a valid driving licence (car = 17+, small moped = 16+), valid motor insurance (driving uninsured is a serious criminal offence!), and road tax.",memoryHook:"M-O-T has 3 letters: annual MOT tests start once a vehicle is 3 years old."},{id:"fc-ch5-035",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"When do you legally need a TV Licence in the UK, and who gets one free?",back:"You must have a TV Licence (which covers all devices at one address, though tenants with separate tenancy agreements in shared houses may need individual ones) to watch or record live TV on any channel/service or to use BBC iPlayer. People aged 75 or over who receive Pension Credit get a free TV Licence (and blind people get a 50% discount)."},{id:"fc-ch5-036",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"What is a National Insurance (NI) number, how do new non-UK residents get one, and what does National Insurance pay for?",back:"A unique personal account number ensuring tax and contributions are recorded properly. UK young people receive one automatically just before turning 16; non-UK nationals apply through DWP / Jobcentre Plus (you can start work before it arrives if you have permission to work). NI funds contributory benefits like the State Pension and helps pay for the NHS."},{id:"fc-ch5-037",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"What is PAYE, and who must complete an HMRC Self-Assessment tax return?",back:'PAYE ("Pay As You Earn") is the system where employers automatically deduct Income Tax and National Insurance from employees’ wages and pay HMRC directly. Self-employed people (and others with untaxed income) must pay their own tax via a Self-Assessment tax return.'},{id:"fc-ch5-038",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"What is Council Tax, how is it calculated, and what does it pay for?",back:"A local municipal tax paid by households based on a valuation band of the property’s size/value (with reductions like 25% single-person discount or student exemptions) that funds local council services such as rubbish collection, libraries, schools, local roads, and police/fire services."},{id:"fc-ch5-039",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"How are members of the public selected for Jury Service in the UK?",back:"Jurors are chosen at random from the Electoral Register (anyone on the register aged 18 up to the statutory upper limit: 75 in England & Wales, 65 in Scotland, 70 in NI, must serve if summoned unless exempt or disqualified by a criminal conviction).",memoryHook:"Upper jury age steps up by 5s: Scotland 65 -> Northern Ireland 70 -> England & Wales 75."},{id:"fc-ch5-040",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"What do volunteer school governors (or Parent Councils/School Boards in Scotland) do, and do you have to be a parent to be a governor?",back:"Anyone aged 18 or over from the local community (NOT only parents!) can apply to volunteer as a school governor. Governors play three vital roles: setting the strategic direction of the school, ensuring accountability (monitoring the headteacher), and monitoring financial performance/budgets."},{id:"fc-ch5-041",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"How do local council elections work in the UK?",back:"Towns, cities, and rural areas are governed by democratically elected local authorities (councils); most local council elections take place in May each year."},{id:"fc-ch5-042",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"Which UK criminal offences carry strict rules around weapons, drugs, hate crime, and smoking in public?",back:`• Carrying even a small knife for "self-defence" in public without good reason is a criminal offence.
• Selling/buying/possessing Class A, B, or C illegal drugs is a crime.
• Smoking in enclosed public places and workplaces is illegal across the UK.
• Harassment, racial/religious hate crimes, and antisocial behaviour are punishable offences.`},{id:"fc-ch5-043",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-courts-police-role",front:"Where can UK residents get free, independent advice on legal rights, consumer issues, debt, or solicitors?",back:"Citizens Advice (Citizens Advice Bureau) provides free, confidential advice on rights, debt, benefits, and legal procedures; the Law Society (or Law Society of Scotland / NI) lists qualified solicitors."},{id:"fc-ch5-044",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"What happens at a polling station on Election Day in the UK?",back:"Registered voters receive a poll card beforehand showing their local polling station (open 7:00 am to 10:00 pm; photo ID is required in Great Britain & Northern Ireland), mark their ballot paper in private inside a booth, and place it in a sealed ballot box (or vote by postal/proxy vote). Nobody has the right to make you tell them how you voted!"},{id:"fc-ch5-045",chapterId:"ch5",subtopicId:"ch5-parliament-elections",visualId:"parliament-courts",front:"What happens after a General Election if NO single political party wins a majority of MPs in the House of Commons?",back:'This is called a "hung parliament", two or more parties may join forces to form a "coalition government" (like in 2010), or the largest party may try to govern as a minority government.'}],a=[{id:"fc-ch3-001",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who were the earliest inhabitants of Britain, and roughly when did Britain become permanently separated from mainland Europe by the Channel?",back:"The first people in Britain were hunter-gatherers in the Stone Age; Britain only became permanently separated from the continent by the Channel about 10,000 years ago.",memoryHook:"Think 10 Downing Street: 10,000 years since Britain got its own island address cut off from mainland Europe (start of the 10k Channel, 6k Farmers, 4k Bronze countdown)."},{id:"fc-ch3-002",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",front:"When did the first farmers arrive in Britain, and where did their ancestors probably come from?",back:"Around 6,000 years ago, with ancestors originating from south-east Europe: they built houses, tombs, and monuments on the land.",memoryHook:"6,000 = Six, Seeds, South-East Europe (and 10k Channel minus 4k Bronze = 6k Farmers)."},{id:"fc-ch3-003",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What is Stonehenge, and in which English county does it stand?",back:"A famous Stone Age (Neolithic) monument in Wiltshire that was probably a special gathering place for seasonal ceremonies."},{id:"fc-ch3-004",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What is Skara Brae, and where is it located?",back:"Located on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland, Skara Brae is the best-preserved prehistoric village in northern Europe.",memoryHook:'"Brae" is a Scots word (hillside), locking Skara Brae to Scotland (Orkney, off the north coast).'},{id:"fc-ch3-005",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",front:"When did the Bronze Age begin in Britain, and how did Bronze Age people bury their dead?",back:'Around 4,000 years ago, when people learned to make bronze; they lived in roundhouses, crafted bronze tools/weapons and gold ornaments, and buried their dead in tombs called "round barrows".',memoryHook:"4 letters in GOLD (first gold ornaments) = 4,000 years ago; B for Bronze = B for round Barrows."},{id:"fc-ch3-006",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",front:"What characterised Iron Age Britain, and what is Maiden Castle?",back:"People made iron weapons/tools, spoke a language of the Celtic family, minted the first coins in Britain (inscribed with the names of Iron Age kings!), and defended impressive hill forts such as Maiden Castle in Dorset.",memoryHook:"Exam trap: the first coins minted in Britain (with kings' names) date to the Iron Age, BEFORE the Romans arrived."},{id:"fc-ch3-007",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"When did Julius Caesar lead a Roman invasion of Britain, and did it succeed?",back:"Julius Caesar led a Roman invasion in 55 BC (and returned briefly in 54 BC), which was unsuccessful: Britain remained separate from the Roman Empire for nearly 100 years."},{id:"fc-ch3-008",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Which Roman Emperor led the successful invasion of Britain in AD 43?",back:"Emperor Claudius led the Roman army in a new invasion in AD 43, conquering almost all of Britain despite resistance from some British tribes.",memoryHook:"Exam trap: Julius Caesar FAILED in 55 BC; Claudius Conquered in AD 43 (countdown digits 4-3)."},{id:"fc-ch3-009",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",front:"Who was Boudicca, which tribe did she lead against the Romans, and where is her famous statue today?",back:"Boudicca was Queen of the Iceni tribe in what is now eastern England, one of the tribal leaders who fought fiercely against the Romans; her bronze statue stands on Westminster Bridge in London."},{id:"fc-ch3-010",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Why did Emperor Hadrian build Hadrian’s Wall across northern England, and what are two famous Roman forts along it?",back:"Areas of modern Scotland were never conquered by the Romans, so Emperor Hadrian built a wall in the north of England to keep out the Picts (ancestors of the Scottish people). Famous forts on the UNESCO World Heritage wall include Housesteads and Vindolanda."},{id:"fc-ch3-011",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-early-roman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"How long did the Romans stay in Britain, when did the Roman army leave, and what did they leave behind?",back:"The Romans remained for nearly 400 years until the Roman army left in AD 410 to defend other parts of the Roman Empire, never to return. They built roads, public buildings, a structure of law, introduced new plants/animals, and saw the first Christian communities appear in the 3rd and 4th centuries AD."},{id:"fc-ch3-012",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Which three northern European tribes invaded Britain after the Romans left in AD 410, and which parts remained free of their rule?",back:"The Jutes, the Angles, and the Saxons (whose languages are the basis of modern English). By about AD 600 Anglo-Saxon kingdoms were established across Britain, while parts of the west: including much of Wales and Scotland, remained free of Anglo-Saxon rule.",memoryHook:'Acronym "JAS" for the three post-Roman tribes: Jutes, Angles, Saxons.'},{id:"fc-ch3-013",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",front:"What famous Anglo-Saxon archaeological treasure was discovered at Sutton Hoo in modern Suffolk?",back:"The burial place of one of the Anglo-Saxon kings, buried with treasure and armour (including an iconic ornate helmet) all placed inside a ship covered by a mound of earth.",memoryHook:"Four S's lock it together: Sutton Hoo, Suffolk, Saxon king's Ship burial."},{id:"fc-ch3-014",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",front:"Who were St Patrick, St Columba, and St Augustine in spreading Christianity across Britain and Ireland?",back:`• St Patrick became patron saint of Ireland.
• St Columba founded a monastery on the island of Iona, off the coast of Scotland.
• St Augustine led missionaries from Rome to southern England and became the FIRST Archbishop of Canterbury!`,memoryHook:"Don't mix up the two missionaries: A = St Augustine, first Archbishop of Canterbury (south); Columba = monastery on Iona off Scotland (north)."},{id:"fc-ch3-015",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'Where did the Vikings come from, when did they first raid Britain, and what was the "Danelaw"?',back:"The Vikings came from Denmark and Norway. They first visited Britain in AD 789 to raid coastal towns and take away goods and slaves, later staying to form their own communities in the east and north of England in an area known as the Danelaw.",memoryHook:"Sequential counting digits 7-8-9 (AD 789) mark the first Viking raids on Britain."},{id:"fc-ch3-016",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Which ruler united the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms of England and defeated the Vikings?",back:"King Alfred the Great, after his victory, many Viking invaders stayed peacefully in the Danelaw, mixed with local communities, and converted to Christianity."},{id:"fc-ch3-017",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",front:"Which king united the peoples of Scotland under one ruler as Viking attacks encouraged unity in the north?",back:'Kenneth MacAlpin, around the same era the term "Scotland" began to be used to describe that country.'},{id:"fc-ch3-018",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",front:"Who was Cnut (also called Canute)?",back:"The first of a short line of Danish kings who briefly ruled England during the Anglo-Saxon era."},{id:"fc-ch3-019",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What happened in 1066 at the Battle of Hastings, and why is 1066 unique in English history?",back:'William, the Duke of Normandy (in northern France), defeated Harold, the Saxon king of England, who was killed in the battle. William became King of England ("William the Conqueror"), and the Norman Conquest of 1066 was the LAST successful foreign invasion of England!'},{id:"fc-ch3-020",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",front:"What is the Bayeux Tapestry?",back:"A famous piece of embroidered linen nearly 70 metres (230 feet) long commemorating the 1066 Battle of Hastings, which can still be seen in France today.",memoryHook:"Exam trap: Bayeux is a town in Normandy, so the tapestry is still seen in France today (not in the British Museum or Tower of London)."},{id:"fc-ch3-021",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What was the Domesday Book, and who ordered it to be written?",back:"Ordered by William the Conqueror, the Domesday Book listed all the towns and villages in England, who lived there, who owned the land, and what animals they owned: giving a picture of society just after the Norman Conquest that still exists today."},{id:"fc-ch3-022",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-anglo-norman",front:"How did the Norman Conquest change England’s ruling class, language, and neighbouring nations?",back:"Norman French became the language of the new ruling class (adding French words to the Anglo-Saxon language); the Normans initially conquered Wales (though the Welsh gradually won territory back) and fought on the Scottish border without invading Scotland."},{id:"fc-ch3-023",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What happened in 1215 involving King John, and what core principle did the Magna Carta establish?",back:'In 1215, King John was forced by his noblemen to agree to a number of demands in the Magna Carta ("Charter of Liberties"): establishing the idea that even the king is subject to the law, protecting nobility’s rights, and restricting the king’s power to collect taxes or make/change laws without consulting nobles.',memoryHook:"1215 = King John was forced to sign right before 12:15 lunch: even the king is subject to the law."},{id:"fc-ch3-024",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",front:"How did the medieval English Parliament divide into two Houses, and how was the medieval Scottish Parliament structured?",back:`• In England: House of Lords (nobility, great landowners, and bishops) and House of Commons (knights who were smaller landowners, and wealthy people from towns and cities).
• In Scotland: Parliament had THREE Houses called "Estates", the lords, the clergy, and the towns.`,memoryHook:`Letter trick: the capital E in "Estates" has 3 horizontal bars for Scotland's 3 Estates (Lords, Clergy, Towns), vs England's 2 Houses.`},{id:"fc-ch3-025",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",front:"How did the development of English law differ from Scottish law during the Middle Ages?",back:'In England, judges developed "common law" through a process of precedence (following previous decisions) and tradition. In Scotland, the legal system developed slightly differently and laws were "codified" (written down).'},{id:"fc-ch3-026",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What did the Statute of Rhuddlan do in 1284, and which famous Welsh castles were built to maintain royal power?",back:"In 1284, King Edward I of England introduced the Statute of Rhuddlan, which annexed Wales to the Crown of England. Huge castles: including Conwy and Caernarfon, were built to maintain this power (by the middle of the 15th century the last Welsh rebellions had been defeated).",memoryHook:"Exam contrast: Edward I annexed Wales to the Crown in 1284 (digits 1-2-8-4 are powers of 2); Henry VIII later passed the Tudor laws giving Wales MPs in London."},{id:"fc-ch3-027",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What happened in 1314 at the Battle of Bannockburn?",back:"The Scottish, led by King Robert the Bruce, defeated the English army of Edward II at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314: ensuring Scotland remained unconquered by the English."},{id:"fc-ch3-028",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",front:'What was "the Pale" in medieval Ireland, and what foreign wars did English kings fight abroad in the Middle Ages?',back:'In the Middle Ages the English ruled an area around Dublin known as "the Pale" (having first gone to help the Irish king of Leinster in 1200, while English lords ruled elsewhere in Ireland). Abroad, medieval knights fought in the Crusades for Christianity in the Holy Land and in the Hundred Years War with France.',memoryHook:'Idiom link: the phrase "beyond the Pale" comes directly from stepping outside this English-ruled boundary around medieval Dublin.'},{id:"fc-ch3-029",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"When did the Black Death strike Britain, what fraction of the population died, and how did it transform medieval society?",back:'In 1348, the Black Death (a form of plague) killed ONE THIRD of the population in England and similar proportions in Scotland and Wales. Resulting labour shortages meant peasants could demand higher wages, weakening feudalism ("serfdom"), creating new social classes like the "gentry" (landowners), and drawing people into towns.',memoryHook:`Digit anchor: 1348 starts with "13", locking in that 1/3 of Britain's population died (not 1/2 or 1/4).`},{id:"fc-ch3-030",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"How long did the Hundred Years War actually last, and who won the famous Battle of Agincourt in 1415?",back:"The Hundred Years War with France actually lasted 116 years! One of its most famous battles was the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, where King Henry V’s vastly outnumbered English army defeated the French (the English mostly left France in the 1450s).",memoryHook:"Digit anchor: in 1415, the first two digits add up to 5 (1 + 4 = 5) for King Henry V at Agincourt."},{id:"fc-ch3-031",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",front:"By AD 1400, how had language changed in England, and what did Geoffrey Chaucer and John Barbour write?",back:"By 1400, Norman French and Anglo-Saxon had merged into the English language used for official documents and the royal court. Geoffrey Chaucer wrote The Canterbury Tales (poems about pilgrims travelling to Canterbury), while in Scotland John Barbour wrote The Bruce (in Scots) about the Battle of Bannockburn.",memoryHook:"Initial match: C = Chaucer wrote Canterbury Tales; B = Barbour wrote The Bruce (about Bannockburn)."},{id:"fc-ch3-032",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",front:"Who was William Caxton, and which famous poems were among the first books he printed?",back:"William Caxton was the first person in England to print books using a printing press: and Geoffrey Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales was one of the first books he printed!",memoryHook:"Triple-C link: Caxton ran England's first press and printed Chaucer's Canterbury Tales (don't pick Gutenberg on England questions)."},{id:"fc-ch3-033",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",front:"Which medieval castles remain in royal/public use today, and which cathedral is famous for its medieval stained glass?",back:"Windsor Castle and Edinburgh Castle were begun in the Middle Ages and are still in use today; great cathedrals like Lincoln Cathedral were built, and York Minster is famous for its medieval stained-glass windows."},{id:"fc-ch3-034",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What were the Wars of the Roses (1455–1485), and which colour rose belonged to each rival royal house?",back:"A 30-year civil war begun in 1455 to decide who should be king of England, fought between supporters of the House of Lancaster (symbol: a Red Rose) and the House of York (symbol: a White Rose).",memoryHook:"Alphabetical match: Lancaster comes before York (L before Y), and Red comes before White (R before W)."},{id:"fc-ch3-035",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"How did the Wars of the Roses end in 1485 at the Battle of Bosworth Field, and how was the Tudor Rose created?",back:"In 1485 at the Battle of Bosworth Field, King Richard III (House of York) was killed in battle, and Henry Tudor (leader of the House of Lancaster) became King Henry VII: the first Tudor king. Henry VII married Richard’s niece, Elizabeth of York, uniting the two families under the red-and-white Tudor Rose!"},{id:"fc-ch3-036",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"How did King Henry VII strengthen the English monarchy after winning the Wars of the Roses?",back:"Henry VII wanted peace and stability after the civil wars: he deliberately reduced the power of the nobles, strengthened central administration, and built up the monarchy’s financial reserves."},{id:"fc-ch3-037",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Why did King Henry VIII break away from the Church of Rome, and what church did he establish?",back:"When the Pope refused to approve Henry VIII’s divorce from his first wife (Catherine of Aragon) so he could marry again in hopes of a male heir, Henry broke away from Rome, established the Church of England with the King: not the Pope, able to appoint bishops and decide worship, and closed monasteries as Protestantism spread."},{id:"fc-ch3-038",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"Name King Henry VIII’s six wives in exact chronological order and what happened to each.",back:`1. Catherine of Aragon, Divorced (Spanish princess; mother of Mary I)
2. Anne Boleyn: Executed at the Tower of London (English; mother of Elizabeth I)
3. Jane Seymour: Died shortly after giving birth to son Edward VI
4. Anne of Cleves, Divorced (German princess)
5. Catherine Howard: Executed (cousin of Anne Boleyn)
6. Catherine Parr, Survived (widow who outlived Henry)`,memoryHook:'Classic fate rhyme: "Divorced, Beheaded, Died, Divorced, Beheaded, Survived." Name tip: Catherine Parr made "par" at the end and survived.'},{id:"fc-ch3-039",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"How was Wales formally united with England during the reign of Henry VIII?",back:"Under the Act for the Government of Wales, Henry VIII united Wales formally with England: the Welsh sent representatives to the House of Commons in London, the Welsh legal system was reformed, and English became the official language of administration."},{id:"fc-ch3-040",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"What religious book was written during the reign of Henry VIII’s Protestant son, King Edward VI?",back:"Edward VI (who ruled briefly and died at age 15) was strongly Protestant; during his reign, the Book of Common Prayer was written to be used in the Church of England (a version is still used in some churches today).",memoryHook:"Exam trap: Edward VI = Book of Common Prayer; James I = King James Version of the Bible. Don't swap them!"},{id:"fc-ch3-041",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:'Why was Queen Mary I known as "Bloody Mary"?',back:"Mary I (daughter of Catherine of Aragon) was a devout Catholic who tried to restore Roman Catholicism in England and persecuted Protestants (burning nearly 300 at the stake) during her short reign."},{id:"fc-ch3-042",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"What happened in Scotland in 1560 during the Reformation, and what happened to Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots?",back:"In 1560, the predominantly Protestant Scottish Parliament abolished the authority of the Pope in Scotland and outlawed Roman Catholic religious services (establishing a Protestant Church of Scotland, NOT a state church). Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots (only a week old when her father died!) fled to England, gave her throne to her Protestant son James VI, was imprisoned for 20 years on suspicion of plotting against her cousin Elizabeth I, and was executed in 1587.",memoryHook:`Major exam trap: don't confuse Queen Mary I ("Bloody Mary", Henry VIII's daughter who ruled England) with Mary, Queen of Scots (Elizabeth I's cousin, mother of James VI/I, executed in 1587).`},{id:"fc-ch3-043",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"How did Queen Elizabeth I (1558–1603) handle religion, and what famous naval victory occurred in 1588?",back:"Elizabeth I (Protestant daughter of Anne Boleyn) re-established the Church of England, requiring everyone to attend local churches while avoiding investigating people’s private inner beliefs. In 1588, the English fleet defeated the Spanish Armada: a large fleet of ships sent by Catholic Spain to conquer England and restore Catholicism.",memoryHook:"Double-8 naval pair (100 years apart): 1588 Spanish Armada defeated at sea; 1688 William of Orange sailed over in the Glorious Revolution."},{id:"fc-ch3-044",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"Who was Sir Francis Drake, and what was the name of his famous ship?",back:"One of the commanders who fought against the Spanish Armada and a founder of England’s naval tradition; his ship, the Golden Hind, was one of the first to sail right around (circumnavigate) the world. During Elizabeth I’s era, English settlers also began to colonise the eastern coast of America.",memoryHook:`Animal wordplay: a "drake" is a duck and a "hind" is a deer, locking Sir Francis Drake to the Golden Hind (don't pick Nelson's HMS Victory).`},{id:"fc-ch3-045",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"Where was William Shakespeare (1564–1616) born, which London theatre is linked to him, and name four of his famous plays.",back:"Born in Stratford-upon-Avon in England; his plays were performed at the Globe Theatre in London (a modern copy stands near the original site). Famous plays include A Midsummer Night’s Dream, Hamlet, Macbeth, and Romeo and Juliet (plus famous sonnets)."},{id:"fc-ch3-046",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who succeeded Elizabeth I in 1603 when she died unmarried and childless, and what major religious book did he commission?",back:'Her cousin James VI of Scotland became King James I of England, Wales, and Ireland in 1603 (though Scotland remained a separate country with its own Parliament). One key achievement of his reign was a new translation of the Bible into English: the "King James Version" or "Authorised Version".',memoryHook:"Exam trap: 1603 (James VI/I) united only the CROWNS, leaving Scotland a separate country with its own Parliament until the 1707 Act of Union under Queen Anne."},{id:"fc-ch3-047",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:'What was the "Plantation" of Ulster under Elizabeth I and James I?',back:'After suppressing rebellions in Ireland, the English government encouraged Scottish and English Protestants to settle in Ulster (the northern province of Ireland), taking over land from Catholic landholders: settlements known as "plantations" with long-term consequences for Irish history.'},{id:"fc-ch3-048",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What was the Gunpowder Plot of 5 November 1605?",back:"A group of Roman Catholics led by Guy Fawkes plotted to blow up the Houses of Parliament and kill Protestant King James I with a bomb; the plot was uncovered and failed (still marked every 5 November on Bonfire Night).",memoryHook:"Matching 5s: 5 November in 1605 (ends in 05) = Gunpowder Plot against Protestant King James I."},{id:"fc-ch3-049",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:'What was the "Divine Right of Kings", and why did King Charles I clash fiercely with Parliament and Scotland?',back:'James I and his son Charles I believed in the "Divine Right of Kings": that the king was directly appointed by God and could rule without Parliament’s approval. Charles I ruled for 11 years without recalling Parliament, raised taxes alone, tried to force a revised Prayer Book on the Presbyterian Church in Scotland (sparking a Scottish invasion), and entered the House of Commons to try to arrest 5 parliamentary leaders!'},{id:"fc-ch3-050",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"When did the English Civil War begin, what were the two sides called, and which two battles sealed Parliament’s victory?",back:"Civil war between the king and Parliament began in 1642: the king’s supporters were the Cavaliers, and Parliament’s supporters were the Roundheads. The Parliamentary army defeated Charles I at the Battles of Marston Moor and Naseby (by 1646 it was clear Parliament had won).",memoryHook:"Alliteration: C = Cavaliers fought for Charles I and the Crown; Roundheads fought for Parliament (and created the Republic)."},{id:"fc-ch3-051",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What happened in 1649 after Charles I refused to reach an agreement with Parliament, and who ruled the Republic ("Commonwealth")?',back:'Charles I was executed in 1649, and England declared itself a republic called the Commonwealth (with no monarch!). General Oliver Cromwell crushed revolts in Ireland and defeated the Scots/Charles II at Dunbar and Worcester (where Charles II famously hid in an oak tree before escaping to Europe). Cromwell was later given the title "Lord Protector" and ruled until his death in 1658.'},{id:"fc-ch3-052",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What was "The Restoration" of May 1660?',back:"After Oliver Cromwell died in 1658 and his son Richard failed to provide clear leadership, Parliament invited Charles II back from exile in the Netherlands to be crowned King Charles II of England, Wales, Scotland, and Ireland in May 1660."},{id:"fc-ch3-053",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Which two disasters struck London in 1665 and 1666, who chronicled them in a famous diary, and who designed the new St Paul’s Cathedral?",back:"In 1665 London suffered a major outbreak of the Great Plague; in 1666 the Great Fire of London destroyed much of the city, including many churches and the old St Paul’s Cathedral. London was rebuilt with a famous new St Paul’s Cathedral designed by Sir Christopher Wren, and Samuel Pepys wrote about these events in his famous published diary.",memoryHook:"1665 Plague came 1 year before triple-6 hellfire (1666 Great Fire). Pepys = Pen (wrote the eyewitness diary); Wren (like the bird) built St Paul's sky-high dome."},{id:"fc-ch3-054",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"What important legal safeguard became law in 1679 under the Habeas Corpus Act?",back:'The Habeas Corpus Act of 1679 (Latin for "you must present the person in court") guaranteed that no one could be held unlawfully as a prisoner: every prisoner has a right to a court hearing.'},{id:"fc-ch3-055",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"What is the Royal Society, and what were the famous discoveries of Edmund Halley and Sir Isaac Newton?",back:'Founded during Charles II’s reign to promote "natural knowledge", the Royal Society is the oldest surviving scientific society in the world. Early members included Sir Edmund Halley (predicted the return of Halley’s Comet) and Sir Isaac Newton (born in Lincolnshire; published Principia Mathematica showing gravity applies across the whole universe, and discovered white light is made up of the colours of the rainbow).'},{id:"fc-ch3-056",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'Why was the event of 1688 called the "Glorious Revolution", and who became joint monarchs?',back:'Catholic King James II (Charles II’s brother) alarmed Protestants by favouring Catholics and arresting bishops. Leading Protestants invited William of Orange (Protestant ruler of the Netherlands, married to James II’s Protestant daughter Mary) to invade England in 1688. There was NO resistance in England: James II fled to France, and William III and Mary II ruled jointly in a bloodless "Glorious Revolution"!'},{id:"fc-ch3-057",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What did the 1689 Bill of Rights establish, and what happened at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690?",back:'The Bill of Rights (1689) confirmed the rights of Parliament and the limits of the king’s power (requiring free elections, frequent Parliaments, Parliamentary consent for taxes/laws, and that the monarch must be Protestant: laying the foundation of constitutional monarchy!). In 1690, William III defeated James II’s French-backed army at the Battle of the Boyne in Ireland (supporters of the Stuarts became known as "Jacobites").',memoryHook:'Consecutive years 1688-1689-1690: Revolution (88), Bill of Rights (89), Boyne (90). Word root: "Jacobus" is Latin for James, so Jacobites were supporters of exiled King James II.'},{id:"fc-ch3-058",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Who were the Huguenots who settled in Britain between 1680 and 1720, and when were newspapers freed from government licensing?",back:"The Huguenots were Protestant refugees fleeing religious persecution in France who brought skills in science, banking, and weaving (following the earlier resettlement of Jews in London in 1656). Also, from 1695 newspapers were allowed to operate without a government licence."},{id:"fc-ch3-059",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What did the Act of Union (Treaty of Union in Scotland) create in 1707, and under which monarch?",back:"Passed in 1707 during the reign of Queen Anne, the Act of Union united England (with Wales) and Scotland into a single Kingdom of Great Britain with one Parliament in London: though Scotland kept its own legal and education systems and its Presbyterian Church.",memoryHook:'Three-step Union ladder: 1603 = Crowns only (James I); 1707 = Great Britain formed with Scotland (Queen Anne); 1801 ("01" = One UK) = Ireland joins to form the UK.'},{id:"fc-ch3-060",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Why did German Hanoverian King George I rely heavily on his ministers, and who became Britain’s FIRST Prime Minister?",back:"When Queen Anne died in 1714 with no surviving children, Parliament chose her nearest Protestant relative: George, Elector of Hanover, as King George I. Because he did not speak very good English, he relied more on his ministers; the most important minister in Parliament became known as the Prime Minister. The first man to be called this was Sir Robert Walpole, Prime Minister from 1721 to 1742!",memoryHook:'Walpole = "pole position" as 1st Prime Minister, and his dates double: 1721 to 1742 (21 + 21 = 42).'},{id:"fc-ch3-061",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What happened in 1746 at the Battle of Culloden, and what were the "Highland Clearances"?',back:'In 1745, Charles Edward Stuart ("Bonnie Prince Charlie", grandson of James II) landed in Scotland supported by Highland clansmen to reclaim the throne, but was defeated by George II’s army at the Battle of Culloden in 1746 (escaping back to Europe). Afterwards, clans lost power as Scottish landlords destroyed small individual farms ("crofts") to make space for sheep and cattle: evictions known as the Highland Clearances, sending many Scots to North America.'},{id:"fc-ch3-062",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Who were the three standout thinkers/inventors of the 18th-century Scottish Enlightenment: Adam Smith, David Hume, and James Watt?",back:`• Adam Smith: developed modern ideas about economics still referred to today (The Wealth of Nations).
• David Hume: influential philosopher whose ideas on human nature continue to shape philosophy.
• James Watt: scientific discoveries and improvements on steam power helped drive the Industrial Revolution.`,memoryHook:"Word stems: Hume = Human nature (philosophy); Watt = Watts of power (steam); Adam Smith = Wealth of Nations (economics)."},{id:"fc-ch3-063",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Who was Richard Arkwright (1732–1792), and what made him famous during the Industrial Revolution?",back:"Originally a barber and wig-maker, Richard Arkwright improved the carding machine (preparing fibres for spinning into yarn and cloth), used horse and water power for mechanised textiles, and is remembered for running efficient, profitable factories.",memoryHook:"Think hair-combing to fibre-combing: barber/wig-maker Arkwright improved the carding machine (combing fibres for spinning) and pioneered efficient factories."},{id:"fc-ch3-064",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"What was the Bessemer process, and how did canals and steam transform 18th- and 19th-century Britain?",back:"Britain was the first country to industrialise on a large scale, fuelled by coal and iron deposits: the Bessemer process enabled the mass production of steel (for shipbuilding and railways), while a network of canals linked factories in the Midlands and north to ports."},{id:"fc-ch3-065",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Who was Sake Dean Mahomed (1759–1851), and what two cultural firsts did he introduce to Britain?",back:'Born in the Bengal region of India, he came to Britain in 1782, eloped with Irish girl Jane Daly, opened the Hindoostane Coffee House in George Street, London in 1810 (the FIRST curry house to open in Britain!), and with his wife introduced "shampooing": the Indian art of head massage!'},{id:"fc-ch3-066",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Which religious group set up the first formal anti-slavery groups in Britain, and which MP led the campaign in Parliament?",back:"The Quakers set up the first formal anti-slavery groups in the late 1700s, petitioning Parliament to ban the trade. William Wilberforce, an evangelical Christian and Member of Parliament, played a vital part in turning public opinion and changing the law."},{id:"fc-ch3-067",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Exam Favourite: Distinguish the 1807 Slave Trade Act from the 1833 Emancipation Act.",back:`• 1807: It became illegal to trade slaves in British ships or from British ports (and the Royal Navy patrolled to stop slave ships from other nations).
• 1833: The Emancipation Act abolished slavery itself throughout the entire British Empire! (After 1833, 2 million Indian and Chinese workers were employed to replace freed slaves on plantations).`,memoryHook:'1807 ("07" = Bond on deck) banned slave ships and trade; 1833 (33 rhymes with "free") abolished slavery itself across the Empire.'},{id:"fc-ch3-068",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"What happened in 1776 in British North America?",back:"After clashes with the British government over taxation without representation, 13 American colonies declared their independence in 1776: defeating the British army in the War of Independence and having independence recognised by Britain in 1783."},{id:"fc-ch3-069",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What did the Act of Union of 1800 (taking effect in 1801) create?",back:"It abolished the separate Irish Parliament and united Ireland with Great Britain, creating the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland: and adding the red diagonal Cross of St Patrick to create the current official Union Flag!",memoryHook:`1801 ends in "01": united Great Britain and Ireland into ONE United Kingdom (and added St Patrick's red diagonal cross to complete today's Union Flag).`},{id:"fc-ch3-070",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who won the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805, what was his flagship called, and where can you visit it today?",back:"Admiral Horatio Nelson led Britain’s navy to defeat the combined French and Spanish fleets at the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805 (Nelson was killed in the battle). Nelson’s Column in Trafalgar Square, London honours him, and his ship, HMS Victory, can still be visited in Portsmouth!",memoryHook:"Three-ship exam trap: Drake = Golden Hind (Elizabethan), Nelson = HMS Victory at Portsmouth (1805 Trafalgar), Brunel = SS Great Britain at Bristol (Victorian steamship)."},{id:"fc-ch3-071",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who defeated French Emperor Napoleon in 1815 at the Battle of Waterloo?",back:'The British army led by the Duke of Wellington ("the Iron Duke", who later became Prime Minister) defeated Emperor Napoleon at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815, ending the Napoleonic Wars.',memoryHook:"W = Wellington won Waterloo (ironically on LAND, 1815), 10 years after N = Nelson won for the Navy at Trafalgar (1805)."},{id:"fc-ch3-072",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:'What did the Great Reform Act of 1832 do, and who were the "Chartists"?',back:'The Reform Act of 1832 greatly increased the number of (male) voters, abolished old pocket and "rotten" boroughs with tiny electorates, and gave more parliamentary seats to growing industrial towns and cities. Working-class campaigners called Chartists then demanded universal male votes, annual elections, secret ballots, equal constituencies, and MPs’ salaries: initially failing, though most goals were adopted by 1918.'},{id:"fc-ch3-073",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"During which years did Queen Victoria reign, and how large did the British Empire grow?",back:"Queen Victoria became queen in 1837 at age 18 and reigned until 1901: almost 64 years! During the Victorian Age, Britain led the world in trade and industry, and the British Empire grew to become the largest empire the world had ever seen (an estimated 400 million people)."},{id:"fc-ch3-074",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"What were the Corn Laws repealed in 1846, and what tragedy struck Ireland in the mid-19th century?",back:"The Corn Laws had prevented the import of cheap grain; their repeal in 1846 promoted free trade and cheaper food/raw materials for factories. Around the same time in the mid-19th century, the potato crop failed in Ireland: a million people died from disease and starvation in the Irish Famine, and a million and a half emigrated (many to the US and English cities like Liverpool, London, Manchester, and Glasgow)."},{id:"fc-ch3-075",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Where was the Great Exhibition of 1851 held, and what building was constructed to house it?",back:"Opened in 1851 in Hyde Park, London inside the Crystal Palace, a huge building made of iron and glass: exhibiting objects from all over the world, most of them made in Britain.",memoryHook:"Exam trap: although Crystal Palace is now a South London district (where the glass building was later moved), the 1851 Great Exhibition itself stood in Hyde Park."},{id:"fc-ch3-076",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Compare Victorian railway and engineering pioneers George & Robert Stephenson and Isambard Kingdom Brunel.",back:`• George & Robert Stephenson: pioneered the railway engine, built the famous locomotive Rocket just before the first passenger railways opened in 1830.
• Isambard Kingdom Brunel (born in Portsmouth): engineer who built tunnels, bridges, railway lines, and ships, including the Great Western Railway (Paddington to SW England/Wales), the Clifton Suspension Bridge over the Avon Gorge in Bristol, and the iron steamship SS Great Britain.`,memoryHook:"Don't swap the engineers: B = Brunel built Bridges (Clifton in Bristol), SS Great Britain, and the Great Western Railway; Stephensons built the Rocket locomotive engine."},{id:"fc-ch3-077",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who fought in the Crimean War (1853–1856), why was its media coverage historic, and what medal did Queen Victoria create during it?",back:"Britain fought alongside Turkey and France against Russia (1853–1856). It was the FIRST war to be extensively covered by the media through news stories and photographs! Queen Victoria also introduced the Victoria Cross medal during this war to honour acts of valour by soldiers."},{id:"fc-ch3-078",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Where was Florence Nightingale (1820–1910) born, where did she nurse soldiers in the Crimean War, and what school did she open in 1860?",back:"Born in Italy to English parents, Florence Nightingale trained as a nurse in Germany (age 31) and in 1854 went to Turkey to treat soldiers at the military hospital in Scutari: dramatically slashing mortality rates through hygiene. In 1860 she established the Nightingale Training School for nurses at St Thomas’ Hospital in London; she is regarded as the founder of modern nursing.",memoryHook:'Her first name encodes her birthplace: "Florence" Nightingale was literally born in Florence, Italy (not Britain!).'},{id:"fc-ch3-079",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"What did the Married Women’s Property Acts of 1870 and 1882 change, and who was Rudyard Kipling (1865–1936)?",back:`• Until 1870, when a woman married, her earnings and property automatically belonged to her husband; the Acts of 1870 and 1882 gave married women the right to keep their own earnings and property!
• Rudyard Kipling (born in India; awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1907) wrote books and poems set in India and the UK reflecting Empire confidence, including The Jungle Book, Just So Stories, and the poem "If".`},{id:"fc-ch3-080",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'Who was Emmeline Pankhurst (1858–1928), when did she found the WSPU ("Suffragettes"), and in which TWO years did women gain the vote?',back:`Born in Manchester in 1858, Emmeline Pankhurst helped found the Women’s Social and Political Union (WSPU) in 1903, whose members: the "Suffragettes", used civil disobedience (chaining themselves to railings, smashing windows, going on hunger strike) to win votes for women.
• 1918: Women aged 30 and over gained the right to vote (recognising women’s WWI effort).
• 1928: Women gained the vote at age 21, equal with men (shortly before Emmeline died)!`,memoryHook:"1918 gave women the vote at age 30+ (after WWI); add 10 years (1928) to drop the voting age to 21, equal with men."},{id:"fc-ch3-081",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",front:"Who fought in the Boer War of 1899 to 1902, and how did it reshape thinking about the British Empire?",back:"British forces went to war in South Africa with settlers from the Netherlands called the Boers (1899–1902). Because the Boers fought fiercely and sympathy grew for them, public debate began over whether the Empire could continue, shifting slowly toward greater self-government for Dominions like Australia, Canada, New Zealand, and South Africa."},{id:"fc-ch3-082",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What event on 28 June 1914 triggered the First World War (1914–1918), and how many British casualties were suffered overall?",back:"The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria set off a chain of events leading to WWI (1914–1918), where Britain and the Allied Powers (including France, Russia, Japan, Belgium, Serbia, and later Italy & the US: plus over 1 million Indians and troops from across the Empire) fought the Central Powers (mainly Germany, the Austro-Hungarian Empire, the Ottoman Empire, and Bulgaria). There were more than 2 million British casualties."},{id:"fc-ch3-083",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Exam Classic: What happened on 1 July 1916 during the Battle of the Somme?",back:"The British attack at the Battle of the Somme in July 1916 resulted in about 60,000 British casualties (dead and wounded) on the FIRST DAY ALONE.",memoryHook:"Digit anchor: July 1916 ends in 6, matching 60,000 British casualties on the first day alone."},{id:"fc-ch3-084",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"At what exact time and date did the First World War end?",back:"At 11:00 am on 11 November 1918, victory for Britain and its allies (commemorated as Armistice / Remembrance Day).",memoryHook:"Triple-11: the 11th hour of the 11th day of the 11th month (1918)."},{id:"fc-ch3-085",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Trace the key events in Ireland from the 1916 Easter Rising to the 1922 partition of Ireland.",back:'In 1913 the British government promised "Home Rule" for Ireland (opposed by Protestants in the north), postponed due to WWI. In 1916, Irish Nationalists staged the Easter Rising against the British in Dublin, followed by guerrilla war. A 1921 peace treaty led in 1922 to Ireland splitting into two countries: the 6 mainly Protestant counties in the north remained in the UK as Northern Ireland, while the rest became the Irish Free State (which became a republic in 1949).',memoryHook:'Double-2 anchor: in 1922 ("22"), Ireland split into 2 countries (Northern Ireland and the Irish Free State, which only became a Republic later in 1949).'},{id:"fc-ch3-086",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",front:"What happened in Britain during the 1926 General Strike and the 1930s Great Depression?",back:'After high unemployment in the 1920s led to the 1926 General Strike, the world entered the "Great Depression" in 1929. Traditional heavy industries (such as shipbuilding in Scotland, north-east England, and Belfast, plus coal and steel) suffered badly, while new light industries: especially car manufacturing and aviation, developed alongside lower prices and a major 1930s house-building boom.'},{id:"fc-ch3-087",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who discovered penicillin in 1928, and who turned it into a usable mass-produced drug?",back:"Scottish doctor Alexander Fleming (researching influenza in London) discovered penicillin in 1928. It was further developed into a usable antibiotic drug by scientists Howard Florey and Ernst Chain by the 1940s; Fleming won the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1945."},{id:"fc-ch3-088",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"When did the BBC begin regular radio broadcasts, and when did it launch the world’s FIRST regular television service?",back:"The BBC started regular radio broadcasts in 1922 and began the world’s first regular television service in 1936! (Also in the 1930s, writers like Graham Greene and Evelyn Waugh flourished, economist John Maynard Keynes published influential theories, and the Daily Mail had earlier pioneered mass popular journalism in 1896).",memoryHook:"Exam contrast: John Logie Baird invented TV in the 1920s, while the BBC launched the world's first regular TV service in 1936 (14 years after starting radio in 1922)."},{id:"fc-ch3-089",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What action by Adolf Hitler caused Britain and France to declare war in 1939, and which nations formed the "Axis" powers?',back:"Adolf Hitler came to power in Germany in 1933; when Hitler invaded Poland in September 1939 (after occupying Austria and invading Czechoslovakia), Britain and France declared war to stop his aggression. The Axis powers were Fascist Germany and Italy plus the Empire of Japan."},{id:"fc-ch3-090",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What was the evacuation of Dunkirk in 1940, and how many men were rescued?",back:'After German forces defeated Allied troops and took control of France and Belgium in spring 1940, many civilian volunteers in small pleasure and fishing boats helped the Royal Navy rescue more than 300,000 British and French soldiers from the beaches around Dunkirk: giving rise to the phrase "the Dunkirk spirit".'},{id:"fc-ch3-091",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"When did Winston Churchill become Prime Minister, what poll did he win in 2002, and what are three of his most famous wartime speech lines?",back:`Winston Churchill (Conservative MP, son of a politician, former journalist/soldier) became Prime Minister in May 1940, refusing to surrender to the Nazis (and was voted the greatest Briton of all time by the public in 2002!). Famous 1940 quotes:
• "I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat"
• "We shall fight on the beaches... we shall never surrender"
• "Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few" (praising RAF pilots!)`},{id:"fc-ch3-092",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What was the Battle of Britain (summer 1940), and which two British fighter planes were most important to the RAF’s victory?",back:"From the end of June until October 1940, the Royal Air Force (RAF) won the crucial aerial battle against the Germans (the Battle of Britain), preventing Germany from invading Britain across the Channel. The two most important British planes used by the RAF were the Spitfire and the Hurricane (designed and built in Britain!).",memoryHook:"Exam trap: pick the two 1940 fighter planes, Spitfire and Hurricane (don't fall for distractors like the Lancaster bomber or 1960s Harrier jump jet)."},{id:"fc-ch3-093",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",front:'What was "The Blitz", and which English Midlands city was almost totally destroyed alongside heavy damage in London?',back:'Despite losing the Battle of Britain, the German air force continued bombing London and other British cities at night: called the Blitz. Coventry was almost totally destroyed, and great damage was caused across London (especially in the East End), inspiring the resilient "Blitz spirit".'},{id:"fc-ch3-094",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",front:"How did the United States and the Soviet Union enter World War II in 1941?",back:"Germany invaded the Soviet Union (where German forces were eventually pushed back with colossal losses on both sides), and the United States entered the war when the Japanese bombed its naval base at Pearl Harbor in December 1941 (around the same time Japan invaded Singapore and Burma)."},{id:"fc-ch3-095",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What happened on 6 June 1944 ("D-Day")?',back:'On 6 June 1944, Allied forces landed in Normandy (northern France) in an event often referred to as "D-Day". Following victory on the Normandy beaches, Allied armies pressed on through France and eventually into Germany, comprehensively defeating Germany in May 1945.',memoryHook:"Double-digit symmetry: 6/6/44 (6th day, 6th month, year '44) = D-Day Normandy landings."},{id:"fc-ch3-096",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",front:'How did the war against Japan end in August 1945, and which scientist working in Britain had earlier been the first to "split the atom"?',back:'The war against Japan ended in August 1945 when the United States dropped atomic bombs on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Earlier, scientists led by Ernest Rutherford (born in New Zealand), working at Manchester and then Cambridge University, were the FIRST to "split the atom": and British scientists later took part in the Manhattan Project in the US.'},{id:"fc-ch3-097",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who won the 1945 General Election right after WWII in Europe ended, and what industries did his government nationalise?",back:"Although Churchill was celebrated as a wartime leader, the British public elected a Labour government in 1945 led by Prime Minister Clement Attlee, promising to implement the Beveridge social reforms and taking coal mines, steel, railways, gas, electricity, and the Bank of England into public ownership (nationalisation)."},{id:"fc-ch3-098",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What was the 1942 Beveridge Report, who wrote it, and what were the "Five Giant Evils" it aimed to fight?',back:'Economist William Beveridge produced the 1942 report Social Insurance and Allied Services (the "Beveridge Report"), commissioned by the wartime government in 1941. It recommended that the government fight the five "Giant Evils" of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor, and Idleness and provide a basic welfare state for all citizens.',memoryHook:'Match each archaic "Giant" to a modern need: Want (poverty), Disease (health), Ignorance (education), Squalor (slum housing), Idleness (unemployment).'},{id:"fc-ch3-099",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:'What did R.A. ("Rab") Butler’s Education Act of 1944 ("The Butler Act") introduce?',back:"Introduced in 1944 by Conservative minister R.A. Butler during the wartime coalition, the Butler Act introduced FREE secondary education in England and Wales and created a clear division between primary and secondary schools."},{id:"fc-ch3-100",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"When was the National Health Service (NHS) established, and which Minister of Health led its creation?",back:'In 1948, Aneurin ("Nye") Bevan, the Labour Minister of Health (under PM Clement Attlee), led the establishment of the National Health Service (NHS), guaranteeing a minimum standard of healthcare for all, free at the point of use! (A national system of "social security" was also introduced).',memoryHook:`"Nye" Bevan = NHS (1948). Don't mix up the three 1940s "B" names: Beveridge (1942 welfare report), Butler (1944 secondary education), Bevan (1948 NHS).`},{id:"fc-ch3-101",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'Which three South Asian nations gained independence from Britain in 1947, and which Prime Minister gave the "Wind of Change" speech?',back:'In 1947, independence was granted to 9 countries, including India, Pakistan, and Ceylon (now Sri Lanka), starting a mostly peaceful 20-year transition from Empire to Commonwealth. Conservative PM Harold Macmillan (1957–1963) gave the famous "Wind of Change" speech about decolonisation and independence in Africa.'},{id:"fc-ch3-102",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Why were workers invited from the West Indies and South Asia to rebuild Britain after WWII, and what happened in 1948 aboard HMT Empire Windrush?",back:"Facing severe post-war labour shortages to rebuild Britain, the government encouraged workers from Ireland, European refugee camps, and the Commonwealth to come and work. In 1948, people from the West Indies were invited to come and work (famously arriving on HMT Empire Windrush), and in the 1950s centres in the West Indies and India/Pakistan (and later Bangladesh) recruited bus drivers, NHS staff, and textile/engineering workers for 25 years."},{id:"fc-ch3-103",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:'What defined Britain’s "Swinging Sixties" in music, fashion, and social laws?',back:'The 1960s ("Swinging Sixties") brought surging pop music (The Beatles from Liverpool and The Rolling Stones), fashion innovations (Mary Quant popularised the miniskirt!), rising wealth (cars & consumer goods), and major liberalised social laws: reforming divorce and abortion laws (in England, Wales & Scotland), improving women’s workplace position, and abolishing capital punishment (the death penalty) for murder in 1965/1969.'},{id:"fc-ch3-104",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:"What was Concorde, which country partnered with Britain to build it, and when did it fly?",back:"Britain and France jointly developed Concorde, the world’s ONLY supersonic passenger airliner! It first flew in 1969, began carrying passengers in 1976, and retired from service in 2003 (alongside other 1960s British aviation triumphs like the Harrier jump jet).",memoryHook:`The French "-e" at the end of "Concorde" ("harmony") points to France as Britain's partner; 1969 (same year as the Moon landing) was its first flight.`},{id:"fc-ch3-105",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"In what year did the UK join the European Economic Community (EEC), and what troubles occurred in the 1970s?",back:'The UK joined the European Economic Community (EEC, precursor to the EU) in 1973 (alongside Ireland and Denmark, under Conservative PM Edward Heath). The late 1970s were marked by economic instability, balance-of-payments problems, strikes, and serious unrest ("the Troubles") in Northern Ireland, where the NI Parliament was suspended in 1972 and around 3,000 people lost their lives over ensuing decades.',memoryHook:"1973 ends in 3: three neighbours (the UK, Ireland, and Denmark) joined the EEC together that year."},{id:"fc-ch3-106",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who became the UK’s FIRST female Prime Minister in 1979, and what major economic changes did her government make?",back:"Margaret Thatcher, leader of the Conservative Party, was elected in 1979 as the UK’s FIRST woman Prime Minister (and longest continuously serving PM of the 20th century, serving until 1990). Her government privatised nationalised industries, imposed legal controls on trade union powers, deregulated financial markets ( sparking huge growth in the City of London), and benefited from North Sea oil and gas revenues."},{id:"fc-ch3-107",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What caused the Falklands War in 1982, and what was the outcome?",back:"In 1982, Argentina invaded the Falkland Islands, a British Overseas Territory in the South Atlantic. A naval taskforce was sent from the UK under Margaret Thatcher’s government, and military action led to the recovery of the islands."},{id:"fc-ch3-108",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:"Who was children’s author Roald Dahl (1916–1990), and who succeeded Margaret Thatcher as Prime Minister in 1990?",back:`• Roald Dahl was born in Wales to Norwegian parents, served in the RAF during WWII, and wrote some of the best-selling children’s books ever published, including Charlie and the Chocolate Factory and George’s Marvellous Medicine.
• John Major succeeded Thatcher as Conservative Prime Minister after her resignation in 1990 and helped establish the Northern Ireland peace process.`,memoryHook:"Exam trap: despite his Norwegian name and parents, Roald Dahl was born in Wales (not Norway or England)."},{id:"fc-ch3-109",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Who led Labour to victory in 1997, and what landmark peace deal was signed in Northern Ireland in 1998?",back:"Tony Blair led the Labour Party elected in 1997. Building on the peace process, the Good Friday Agreement (also called the Belfast Agreement) was signed in 1998, leading to the election of the power-sharing Northern Ireland Assembly in 1999 (and decommissioning of paramilitary groups by 2007).",memoryHook:"Consecutive years 1997-1998-1999: Blair elected (97), Good Friday Agreement signed (98), devolved assemblies opened (99)."},{id:"fc-ch3-110",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'What major constitutional change ("Devolution") did the Labour government introduce in Scotland and Wales in 1999?',back:"Following referendums, the government introduced a Scottish Parliament (with substantial powers to legislate) and a Welsh Assembly (with fewer initial legislative powers, but considerable control over public services: now Senedd Cymru / Welsh Parliament), both opening in 1999!"},{id:"fc-ch3-111",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:"Who became Prime Minister in 2007, and why was the May 2010 UK General Election historically unusual?",back:"Gordon Brown took over from Tony Blair as Labour PM in 2007. In May 2010, for the first time in the UK since 1974, NO political party won an overall majority: resulting in the Conservative and Liberal Democrat parties forming a coalition government with David Cameron as Prime Minister."},{id:"fc-ch3-112",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What global sporting celebration did London host in 2012, and where was the main Olympic Park built?",back:"In 2012, the UK hosted the London 2012 Olympic and Paralympic Games (making London the first city to host the modern Summer Olympics three times: 1908, 1948, and 2012!), with the main Olympic site regenerating Stratford in East London and Team GB finishing 3rd in the Olympic medal table."},{id:"fc-ch3-113",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:'When did the UK vote in a referendum to leave the European Union, and on what exact date did the UK officially leave the EU ("Brexit")?',back:"In a public referendum held on 23 June 2016, the UK voted (51.9% to 48.1%) to leave the European Union. The UK officially left the EU on 31 January 2020.",memoryHook:"Exam trap: June 2016 was only the referendum vote; the UK officially left the EU on 31 January 2020."},{id:"fc-ch3-114",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"What historic reign record did Queen Elizabeth II achieve before her death in 2022, and who succeeded her as monarch?",back:"Queen Elizabeth II reigned for 70 years from 1952 to September 2022, celebrating her Platinum Jubilee in 2022 and surpassing Queen Victoria to become Britain’s longest-reigning monarch in history! She was succeeded upon her death by her eldest son, His Majesty King Charles III."},{id:"fc-ch3-115",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"Which English composer wrote church and theatre music during the late 17th-century Stuart courts of Charles II, James II, and William & Mary?",back:"Henry Purcell (1659–1695), organist at Westminster Abbey who wrote church music, operas (such as Dido and Aeneas), and theatre music that influenced British composers for centuries.",memoryHook:"Exam contrast: Purcell was native English (17th-century Stuart courts, Westminster Abbey); Handel came later from Germany for the 18th-century Georgian kings (Messiah, Water Music)."},{id:"fc-ch3-116",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-tudors-stuarts",front:"What caused the Massacre of Glencoe in Scotland after the 1688 Glorious Revolution?",back:"When a Scottish clan (the MacDonalds of Glencoe) was late in taking the oath of loyalty to King William III due to bad weather, they were killed in the Massacre of Glencoe: causing deep distrust of the new government among Scottish Jacobites."},{id:"fc-ch3-117",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Who was Robert Burns (1759–1796), and what song did he write that is sung across the world at New Year?",back:'Known in Scotland as "The Bard", Scottish poet Robert Burns wrote in the Scots language, English with Scottish words, and standard English: revising old folk songs and writing "Auld Lang Syne", sung when celebrating the New Year (31 December / Hogmanay).'},{id:"fc-ch3-118",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-empire-industry",front:"Who was Lord Shaftesbury, and what social causes did Victorian reformers champion?",back:"During the 19th century, factory and workhouse conditions were harsh (long hours, child labour). Reformers like Lord Shaftesbury campaigned to improve working conditions, restrict child labour, and expand schooling for poor children.",memoryHook:'Name hook: "Shaft"-esbury campaigned to rescue Victorian children from dark mine shafts, factories, and workhouses.'},{id:"fc-ch3-119",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-world-wars",front:"Who were Wilfred Owen and Siegfried Sassoon?",back:'British soldiers and "War Poets" who fought in the trenches of the First World War (1914–1918) and wrote powerful poems describing the harsh human reality and tragedy of war.'},{id:"fc-ch3-120",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:"What international peacekeeping and coalition operations did British armed forces take part in during the 1990s and 2000s?",back:"Since 1945 British armed forces served in NATO and UN missions: in the 1990s Britain played a leading role in the coalition that liberated Kuwait after Iraq’s invasion (1990–91 Gulf War) and intervened in former Yugoslavia (Bosnia and Kosovo); in the 2000s UK forces fought in Afghanistan and Iraq (leaving combat operations in Iraq in 2009)."},{id:"fc-ch3-121",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-modern-britain",front:'What terror attacks struck London on 7 July 2005 ("7/7")?',back:"On 7 July 2005, four coordinated suicide bombings hit London’s public transport system (three Underground trains and a bus in morning rush hour), killing 52 commuters and injuring hundreds."},{id:"fc-ch3-122",chapterId:"ch3",subtopicId:"ch3-middle-ages",visualId:"history-timeline",front:"Quick Timeline Check (Medieval Big 5): Match 1215, 1284, 1314, 1348, and 1415 to their landmark events!",back:`• 1215: Magna Carta (King John)
• 1284: Statute of Rhuddlan (Edward I annexes Wales)
• 1314: Battle of Bannockburn (Robert the Bruce wins for Scotland)
• 1348: The Black Death arrives in Britain (1/3 die)
• 1415: Battle of Agincourt (Henry V defeats French)`,memoryHook:'Quick digit triggers: 12:15 lunch = Magna Carta; 1-2-8-4 (powers of 2) = Wales annexed; "13" in 1348 = 1/3 died in Plague; 1+4=5 in 1415 = Henry V (leaving 1314 = Bannockburn).'}],t=[...e,...a];export{t as B};
